import type { SystemDesignProblem } from "../../types";

export const systemDesignProblems: SystemDesignProblem[] = [
  {
    id: "sd-autocomplete",
    title: "Autocomplete / Search Suggestions System",
    difficulty: "Advanced",
    category: "System Design",
    tags: [
      "search",
      "debouncing",
      "caching",
      "keyboard-navigation",
      "accessibility",
      "trie",
      "api-design",
    ],
    requirements: `The autocomplete system must provide real-time search suggestions as the user types into a search input field. The primary requirement is to display a ranked list of up to 10 suggestions within 100ms of user input, creating a seamless and responsive experience. The system should support prefix matching, fuzzy matching for typo tolerance, and recently searched terms that are personalized per user session.

Beyond basic suggestions, the system must handle multi-language input, support highlighted matching text within suggestions (bolding the matched portion), and allow keyboard navigation through the suggestion list using arrow keys, Enter to select, and Escape to dismiss. Each suggestion item should support different types — recent searches, trending searches, product suggestions, and category suggestions — each rendered with distinct iconography and layout.

The system must integrate with an existing search API backend and gracefully degrade when the API is slow or unavailable. It should also respect user privacy settings, allowing users to clear their search history and opt out of personalized suggestions. Rate limiting on the client side through debouncing and throttling is essential to prevent overwhelming the backend, and the system should track analytics events such as suggestion impressions, clicks, and search completions.`,

    constraints: `The suggestion list must render within 100ms of the last keystroke to feel instantaneous. API calls should be debounced at 150-300ms to balance responsiveness with network efficiency. The frontend must handle up to 1000 requests per minute from a single user session during rapid typing without degrading UI performance.

The suggestion dropdown must not cause layout shifts in the surrounding page content — it should overlay existing content using absolute or fixed positioning with proper z-index management. The component must work correctly on mobile devices where virtual keyboards consume screen real estate, and the dropdown should be positioned to remain visible. Memory usage should stay below 5MB for cached suggestions, with an LRU eviction policy.

Network payloads for suggestions must stay under 2KB per response to ensure fast delivery on slow connections. The system must support cancellation of in-flight requests when the user types a new character (aborting stale requests), and it must handle race conditions where responses arrive out of order. The component must meet WCAG 2.1 AA accessibility standards including proper ARIA attributes and screen reader announcements.`,

    highLevelArchitecture: `The high-level architecture follows a layered approach with clear separation between the UI layer, the data-fetching layer, and the caching layer. At the top, a SearchBar component owns the input field and manages the open/closed state of the dropdown. Below it, a SuggestionList component renders the filtered and ranked suggestions. A dedicated SuggestionService module handles all data fetching, debouncing, caching, and request lifecycle management.

The data flow begins when the user types a character, triggering an onChange event in the SearchBar. This event updates local state and calls the SuggestionService with the current query string. The SuggestionService first checks a multi-tier cache — an in-memory LRU cache for exact prefix matches, then a sessionStorage cache for recently fetched queries. On cache miss, it dispatches an AbortController-managed fetch request to the backend API endpoint (GET /api/suggestions?q={query}&limit=10&type=all). The API returns a ranked array of suggestion objects with type, label, metadata, and a match score.

The architecture includes a PrefetchManager that anticipates common next queries based on the current input. For example, if the user types "reac", the system pre-fetches "react" results in the background. A separate AnalyticsCollector batches and sends impression and click events to the analytics endpoint every 5 seconds or on page unload. The entire flow is orchestrated through a custom useAutocomplete hook that exposes the query, suggestions, loading state, selected index, and handler functions to the UI components.`,

    componentArchitecture: `The React component tree is designed for maximum reusability and testability. The root component is AutocompleteProvider, a context provider that holds the autocomplete configuration (API endpoint, debounce timing, max suggestions, feature flags). Below it sits the SearchBar component, which composes an Input component (controlled, with ref forwarding for focus management), a ClearButton, and the SuggestionDropdown.

The SuggestionDropdown is conditionally rendered based on the isOpen state and contains a SuggestionList. The SuggestionList maps over suggestion items and renders polymorphic SuggestionItem components based on the suggestion type. A RecentSuggestionItem shows a clock icon and a delete button; a TrendingSuggestionItem shows a trending arrow icon; a ProductSuggestionItem shows a thumbnail, title, and price; and a CategorySuggestionItem shows a folder icon with breadcrumb text. Each variant implements a common SuggestionItemProps interface.

Above the suggestion list, there is an optional SuggestionHeader showing "Recent Searches" or "Trending" section labels. Below the list, a SuggestionFooter shows keyboard navigation hints ("↑↓ to navigate, Enter to select, Esc to close"). The entire autocomplete tree is wrapped in a FocusTrap component that manages focus when the dropdown is open and returns focus to the input when dismissed. A VisuallyHidden component announces suggestion count changes to screen readers via aria-live regions.`,

    stateManagement: `State management uses a combination of local component state via useState and useReducer for UI concerns, and a custom hook (useAutocomplete) for the suggestion data lifecycle. The useAutocomplete hook internally uses useReducer with a well-defined state shape: { query: string, suggestions: Suggestion[], isLoading: boolean, error: Error | null, selectedIndex: number, isOpen: boolean, recentSearches: string[] }.

The reducer handles actions like SET_QUERY, SET_SUGGESTIONS, SET_LOADING, SET_ERROR, NAVIGATE_UP, NAVIGATE_DOWN, SELECT_SUGGESTION, OPEN_DROPDOWN, CLOSE_DROPDOWN, CLEAR_RECENT, and REMOVE_RECENT. This centralized state management prevents impossible states — for example, the reducer ensures that isLoading is false when suggestions are set, and selectedIndex resets to -1 when new suggestions arrive.

Recent searches are persisted to localStorage through a useEffect that syncs on changes, with a useLocalStorage hook abstracting the serialization. The debounced query value is derived using a useDebounce hook (150ms default), and API calls are triggered only when the debounced value changes via useEffect. The AbortController for in-flight requests is stored in a useRef to persist across renders without triggering re-renders. Global analytics state (impression tracking) is managed through a lightweight context to avoid prop drilling through the suggestion tree.`,

    apiDesign: `The primary endpoint is GET /api/suggestions with query parameters: q (search query string, required), limit (max results, default 10), type (filter by suggestion type: "all" | "recent" | "trending" | "product" | "category"), locale (language code for i18n), and sessionId (for personalization). The response shape is: { suggestions: Array<{ id: string, type: SuggestionType, label: string, highlightRanges: Array<[number, number]>, metadata: Record<string, unknown>, score: number }>, queryId: string, processingTimeMs: number }.

The highlightRanges array contains character index pairs indicating which portions of the label match the query, enabling precise text highlighting without regex parsing on the client. The queryId is used for analytics attribution — when a user clicks a suggestion, the click event includes the queryId to measure suggestion quality.

A secondary endpoint POST /api/search/analytics accepts batched events: { events: Array<{ type: "impression" | "click" | "search" | "dismiss", queryId: string, suggestionId?: string, position?: number, timestamp: number }> }. A third endpoint DELETE /api/suggestions/recent clears the user's recent search history. The API uses ETag-based caching with a 60-second max-age for trending suggestions and no-cache for personalized results. The client implementation uses a typed API client generated from an OpenAPI spec, with automatic retry (exponential backoff, max 2 retries) on 5xx errors and immediate failure on 4xx errors.`,

    caching: `The caching strategy employs three tiers to minimize API calls while keeping suggestions fresh. The first tier is an in-memory LRU cache (capacity: 100 entries) keyed by the normalized query string (lowercase, trimmed). This cache has a 30-second TTL for personalized results and a 5-minute TTL for trending/general results. Cache hits bypass the network entirely and return suggestions in under 1ms.

The second tier is sessionStorage, which persists across page navigations within the same tab. When a user navigates to a product page and returns to search, their previous query results are instantly available. SessionStorage entries follow the same TTL rules but survive React component unmounts and remounts. The cache key format is "ac:{query}:{type}" to namespace properly.

The third tier is HTTP caching via Cache-Control and ETag headers. The browser's native cache stores API responses with appropriate cache directives set by the server. For cache invalidation, the system uses a hybrid approach: TTL-based expiration for natural freshness, plus a cache-bust parameter based on a version hash that the server includes in the HTML page. When the product catalog changes, the server increments the version hash, and subsequent suggestion requests bypass stale cached responses. Prefix-based cache warming is also implemented — when the user types "sh", the system pre-fetches results for the most common extensions ("sho", "shi", "she") during idle time using requestIdleCallback.`,

    performance: `Performance optimization starts with aggressive debouncing — the system waits 150ms after the last keystroke before making an API call. During this window, if the cache contains a prefix match (e.g., cached results for "rea" are used to locally filter when the user types "reac"), the UI shows filtered cached results immediately while the debounced API call fetches the precise results. This "optimistic local filtering" makes the UI feel instantaneous.

The SuggestionList uses virtualization via a lightweight virtual scroller when the suggestion count exceeds 20 items (for category/product-heavy results). Each SuggestionItem is wrapped in React.memo with a custom areEqual function that checks only id and selectedIndex to prevent unnecessary re-renders during keyboard navigation. The highlight rendering uses a pre-computed range array rather than regex matching, avoiding expensive string operations on each render.

CSS containment (contain: content) is applied to the dropdown to isolate it from the main page's layout and paint calculations. The dropdown uses will-change: transform for GPU-accelerated animations on open/close. Font loading is handled so that suggestion text doesn't cause layout shifts. API responses are compressed with gzip, and the client uses Brotli Accept-Encoding where supported. Request cancellation via AbortController ensures that only the most recent request's response is processed, preventing UI flicker from out-of-order responses. The analytics batching system uses navigator.sendBeacon on page unload to avoid blocking navigation.`,

    security: `The primary security concern is injection attacks through the search query. The system sanitizes all user input before rendering — suggestion labels received from the API are rendered as text nodes (using textContent or React's default JSX escaping), never as innerHTML. The API endpoint performs server-side input validation, rejecting queries exceeding 200 characters or containing suspicious patterns.

Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF) is mitigated by including a CSRF token in the suggestion API headers, sourced from a meta tag rendered by the server. All API requests use the SameSite=Strict cookie attribute. The sessionId parameter used for personalization is a non-reversible hash of the user's session, not the actual session token, preventing session fixation attacks through URL sharing.

Rate limiting is enforced both client-side (debouncing + max 10 requests per second circuit breaker) and server-side (100 requests per minute per IP). The recent searches feature stores data only in localStorage (client-side), never sending historical queries to the server unless the user explicitly enables personalization. Search input is never logged in full to analytics — only the first 50 characters and only after the user presses Enter or clicks a suggestion. The suggestion API endpoint enforces CORS with a strict origin allowlist and uses Content-Security-Policy headers to prevent data exfiltration.`,

    accessibility: `The autocomplete implements the WAI-ARIA combobox pattern (role="combobox") with associated listbox (role="listbox") and option (role="option") roles. The input has aria-expanded reflecting the dropdown open state, aria-controls pointing to the listbox ID, aria-activedescendant set to the currently highlighted option ID during keyboard navigation, and aria-autocomplete="list" indicating the behavior type.

Screen reader announcements use an aria-live="polite" region that announces the number of available suggestions when the list updates (e.g., "8 suggestions available"). When a suggestion is selected via keyboard, the aria-live region announces the selected item. The suggestion list supports full keyboard navigation: ArrowDown/ArrowUp moves the visual highlight and updates aria-activedescendant, Enter selects the current item, Escape closes the dropdown and returns focus to the input, Home/End jumps to the first/last suggestion.

Each SuggestionItem includes descriptive labels beyond the visible text. A product suggestion includes aria-label with the product name, price, and category for context. The delete button on recent searches has aria-label="Remove [query] from recent searches". The entire dropdown respects prefers-reduced-motion — open/close animations are disabled when the user prefers reduced motion. Color contrast ratios for suggestion text, highlight text, and hover/focus states all meet WCAG 2.1 AA standards (4.5:1 minimum for normal text). Touch targets for suggestion items are at least 44x44px on mobile devices.`,

    errorHandling: `Error handling covers network failures, API errors, timeout scenarios, and malformed responses. The useAutocomplete hook maintains an error state that categorizes errors as NETWORK_ERROR (no connectivity), TIMEOUT_ERROR (request exceeded 3-second limit), API_ERROR (4xx/5xx responses), and PARSE_ERROR (malformed JSON). Each error type triggers a different UI response.

For NETWORK_ERROR and TIMEOUT_ERROR, the system shows cached results (if available) with a subtle "Showing cached results" indicator, and retries the request with exponential backoff (1s, 2s, 4s max). If no cached results exist, a non-intrusive inline message replaces the suggestion list: "Unable to load suggestions. Try typing your full search and pressing Enter." The system never blocks the user from performing a regular search even when suggestions fail.

For API_ERROR responses, 429 (rate limited) triggers a client-side backoff that temporarily increases the debounce timer to 500ms. 401/403 errors redirect through the auth refresh flow. 5xx errors fall back to cached or local results. For PARSE_ERROR, the system logs the error to the monitoring service and falls back to showing recent searches only. A circuit breaker pattern is implemented: after 3 consecutive failures within 30 seconds, the system stops making suggestion API calls for 60 seconds and relies entirely on cached and local data, showing a minimal "Suggestions temporarily unavailable" message.`,

    offlineStrategy: `The offline strategy enables basic autocomplete functionality even without network connectivity. A service worker intercepts suggestion API requests and implements a stale-while-revalidate caching strategy — it serves cached responses immediately while attempting a network fetch in the background. The service worker cache stores the last 200 unique query responses with a 24-hour expiration.

When the user goes offline (detected via navigator.onLine and the 'offline' event), the system switches to a local-only mode. In this mode, suggestions are generated from three local sources: the user's recent search history (stored in localStorage), a pre-cached set of popular/trending terms (downloaded during the service worker installation phase, approximately 50KB covering the top 1000 queries), and a client-side prefix trie built from product category names and common search terms bundled with the application.

The transition between online and offline modes is seamless — the SuggestionService abstracts the data source, and the UI shows a subtle wifi-off icon next to suggestions when operating in offline mode. When connectivity returns (detected via the 'online' event and confirmed with a lightweight ping to the API health endpoint), the system gradually transitions back to API-sourced suggestions. Any searches performed while offline are queued and synced to the analytics endpoint when connectivity resumes, ensuring no data loss in usage tracking.`,

    scalability: `At 10x scale (10x more users/queries), the primary frontend concern is the efficiency of the caching layer. The LRU cache size should dynamically adjust based on the device's available memory (using navigator.deviceMemory where available). The pre-fetching strategy should become more selective, only pre-fetching for queries where the cache hit rate data (collected via analytics) indicates high likelihood of subsequent navigation. The debounce timer may need to increase to 200ms to reduce aggregate API load.

At 100x scale, the architecture needs structural changes. A CDN-cached suggestion endpoint for popular queries (the top 10,000 queries, updated hourly) eliminates origin hits for 80%+ of requests. The client detects CDN cache status via response headers and adjusts its caching behavior accordingly. A WebSocket or Server-Sent Events connection can replace polling for real-time trending suggestions, pushing updates to the client rather than requiring repeated fetches.

For extreme scale, the suggestion rendering itself must be optimized. The component tree should be code-split so that autocomplete JS is loaded on-demand when the search input receives focus, reducing initial page load. Suggestion type renderers (product, category, etc.) should be lazy-loaded based on which types the API actually returns. The analytics batching window should widen from 5 seconds to 30 seconds to reduce write amplification on the analytics backend. Internationalization assets should be loaded per-locale rather than bundling all locales, and the trie-based local fallback should support multiple languages via separate downloadable trie files.`,

    tradeoffs: `Debounce timing is the most impactful tradeoff. A shorter debounce (100ms) feels more responsive but generates 2-3x more API calls, increasing server load and cost. A longer debounce (300ms) reduces load but makes the UI feel sluggish, especially on fast connections where the API responds in under 50ms. The chosen 150ms debounce is a compromise — it can be dynamically adjusted based on the user's connection speed (via the Network Information API) and current API latency.

Client-side caching introduces the tradeoff between freshness and speed. Aggressively caching suggestions (long TTLs) means the user might see stale results — a product that's out of stock might still appear in suggestions. Short TTLs maintain freshness but negate the caching benefit. The hybrid approach (short TTL for personalized, longer for trending) adds complexity. Similarly, the prefix-based local filtering (showing filtered cached results for "reac" from "rea" cache) trades accuracy for perceived speed — the locally filtered results might not match the API's ranking.

The combobox accessibility pattern forces certain UX decisions. ARIA requires aria-activedescendant to be set on the input, which means focus must remain on the input during keyboard navigation — this conflicts with some designs that want focus to move into the suggestion list. Using role="listbox" limits each suggestion to role="option", which cannot contain interactive elements (like a delete button on recent searches). The workaround of placing delete buttons outside the option role adds DOM complexity. The choice between a controlled and uncontrolled input pattern also matters: controlled gives precise state management but may cause input lag on slow devices, while uncontrolled is faster but harder to synchronize with the suggestion lifecycle.`,
  },
  {
    id: "sd-news-feed",
    title: "News Feed System",
    difficulty: "Advanced",
    category: "System Design",
    tags: [
      "infinite-scroll",
      "real-time",
      "optimistic-ui",
      "content-ranking",
      "virtualization",
      "feed-algorithm",
    ],
    requirements: `The news feed system must display a continuously updating stream of content items including text posts, images, videos, shared links, and user interactions (likes, comments, shares). The primary feed should support infinite scrolling with smooth loading of new content as the user scrolls down. New content should appear in near-real-time (within 5 seconds of creation) when the user is viewing the top of their feed, with a "New posts available" banner rather than disruptive auto-insertion.

Each feed item must support rich interactions: like/react (with multiple reaction types), comment (with nested replies), share/repost, bookmark, and report. Interactions should use optimistic UI updates — the like count increments immediately on click, and if the server request fails, the UI reverts with a toast notification. The feed must support content ranking based on relevance (engagement score, recency, author relationship) with the option to switch between "Top" and "Latest" sorting modes.

Content moderation must be integrated into the feed rendering pipeline. NSFW content is blurred by default with a click-to-reveal interaction. Reported content is hidden and replaced with a feedback prompt. The feed must support embedded media with lazy loading, responsive image sizing (srcset), and video autoplay-on-scroll with muted audio. The system should track scroll position and reading time for analytics, and restore scroll position when navigating back to the feed from a detail page.`,

    constraints: `Initial feed load must complete within 2 seconds, rendering the first 10 items. Subsequent pages (infinite scroll) should load within 1 second and contain 10-20 items each. The feed must maintain smooth 60fps scrolling even with 1000+ items loaded in the DOM — this requires virtualization. Memory usage should not exceed 100MB even after extended scrolling sessions.

Each feed item can contain up to 5000 characters of text, 10 images, or 1 video. Image payloads should use modern formats (WebP/AVIF with JPEG fallback) and responsive sizing. Video content should use adaptive bitrate streaming (HLS/DASH). The total JavaScript bundle for the feed module should stay under 150KB gzipped.

The system must handle intermittent connectivity — users on mobile connections should not lose their scroll position or unsaved interactions when connectivity drops. The feed API must support cursor-based pagination (not offset-based) to handle content insertion and deletion without skipping or duplicating items. The system must work without JavaScript for initial content rendering (SSR/SSG for SEO), with client-side hydration adding interactivity.`,

    highLevelArchitecture: `The architecture separates the feed into four primary layers: the rendering layer, the data management layer, the real-time synchronization layer, and the analytics layer. The rendering layer consists of React components that handle the visual presentation, virtualization, and user interactions. The data management layer uses a normalized Redux store (or Zustand) for feed items and their relationships, with optimistic update capabilities. The real-time layer manages a WebSocket connection (or SSE fallback) for live updates. The analytics layer collects viewport visibility and interaction data.

Data flows in two directions. The primary flow (fetch-and-render) starts with the FeedContainer requesting the initial page from GET /api/feed?cursor=null&limit=20&sort=top. The response contains serialized feed items with embedded author profiles, engagement counts, and media URLs. Items are normalized into entities (posts, users, media) and stored in the state. The VirtualizedFeedList renders only visible items plus a buffer, pulling data from the store by ID.

The secondary flow (real-time push) starts with a WebSocket connection to ws://api/feed/live?userId={id}. The server pushes events for new posts, engagement updates (like count changes), new comments, and deletions. These events are processed by a FeedEventProcessor that decides whether to insert new items (at top with banner), update existing items (merge engagement counts), or remove items (moderation). A FeedReconciler handles conflicts between optimistic local state and server-pushed state, ensuring consistency.`,

    componentArchitecture: `The component tree is rooted at FeedPage, which contains FeedHeader (sort toggle, filter options), FeedComposer (create new post), NewPostsBanner (notification for new content), VirtualizedFeedList (the main scrollable area), and FeedLoadingFooter (loading spinner for pagination). FeedPage is wrapped in FeedProvider which supplies the feed context (sort mode, active filters, user preferences).

VirtualizedFeedList uses a windowing library (react-window or a custom implementation) and renders FeedItem components. Each FeedItem is a polymorphic component that delegates to PostItem, SharedLinkItem, MediaItem, or RepostItem based on the item type. PostItem contains PostHeader (avatar, author name, timestamp, privacy icon, overflow menu), PostBody (text content with expandable "See more", embedded links with preview cards), PostMedia (responsive image gallery or video player), and PostFooter (engagement counts + action buttons: Like, Comment, Share, Bookmark).

The comment section is lazily loaded — clicking "Comments" triggers a dynamic import of the CommentSection component, which contains CommentList (with nested CommentThread components), CommentComposer, and CommentSortToggle. The Like button uses a ReactionPicker popover (long-press or hover-activated) with animated emoji reactions. Each interactive element is a separate memoized component to isolate re-renders — when a like count changes, only the PostFooter re-renders, not the entire PostItem.`,

    stateManagement: `The feed state architecture uses a normalized entity store pattern. The top-level state shape is: { entities: { posts: Record<string, Post>, users: Record<string, User>, media: Record<string, Media>, comments: Record<string, Comment> }, feed: { itemIds: string[], cursor: string | null, hasMore: boolean, sort: "top" | "latest", isLoading: boolean, error: Error | null }, realtime: { pendingItems: string[], isConnected: boolean }, optimistic: { pendingActions: OptimisticAction[] } }.

Entity normalization is critical for performance. When a user appears in 50 feed items, their profile data is stored once in entities.users and referenced by ID everywhere. When the user updates their avatar, a single entity update propagates to all 50 items. The feed.itemIds array stores only post IDs in display order, keeping the feed slice lightweight. The optimistic slice tracks pending server confirmations — each optimistic action stores the action type, entity ID, previous value, and new value, enabling rollback on failure.

Local UI state (which post has expanded text, which post's comments are visible, which reaction picker is open) is managed with component-level useState to avoid polluting the global store. Scroll position is stored in a ref (not state) to avoid re-renders during scrolling. The infinite scroll trigger uses an IntersectionObserver on a sentinel element near the bottom, dispatching a FETCH_NEXT_PAGE action when intersected. The real-time pending items queue (pendingItems) accumulates new post IDs without inserting them into the feed, and the NewPostsBanner shows their count. Clicking the banner dispatches FLUSH_PENDING_ITEMS, which prepends the pending IDs to feed.itemIds.`,

    apiDesign: `The feed API uses cursor-based pagination via GET /api/feed with parameters: cursor (opaque string from previous response, null for first page), limit (items per page, 10-50), sort ("top" or "latest"), filter (content type filter), and fields (sparse fieldset for partial responses). The response shape is: { items: FeedItem[], nextCursor: string | null, hasMore: boolean, meta: { totalEstimate: number, generatedAt: string } }. Each FeedItem contains: { id, type, author: UserSummary, content: PostContent, media: Media[], engagement: { likes: number, comments: number, shares: number, userReaction: ReactionType | null }, createdAt, updatedAt, privacy, moderationStatus }.

Interaction endpoints use optimistic-friendly designs. POST /api/posts/{id}/reactions { type: "like" | "love" | "laugh" | ... } returns { success: boolean, newCount: number, userReaction: ReactionType }. DELETE /api/posts/{id}/reactions removes the user's reaction. POST /api/posts/{id}/comments { content: string, parentId?: string } returns the created comment with a temporary client-generated ID mapped to the server ID. The client uses the clientId for optimistic rendering and reconciles with the serverId upon response.

The real-time endpoint ws://api/feed/stream sends typed events: { type: "new_post" | "engagement_update" | "comment_added" | "post_deleted" | "moderation_action", payload: unknown, timestamp: number }. Engagement updates are batched — the server sends aggregate count updates every 5 seconds rather than individual events for each like, reducing WebSocket message volume. A REST fallback endpoint GET /api/feed/updates?since={timestamp} supports polling when WebSocket connections are unavailable.`,

    caching: `The caching architecture operates at three levels. The application-level cache stores normalized entities in the Redux/Zustand store, persisted to IndexedDB via redux-persist (or a custom persistence middleware) with entity-level timestamps. When the user returns to the feed, the cached entity data renders immediately while a background revalidation fetch updates stale items. Entity staleness is determined by comparing entity.updatedAt with the last sync timestamp.

Media caching uses the browser's native HTTP cache with aggressive Cache-Control headers (max-age=31536000 for immutable content-addressed images/videos, no-cache for avatar URLs that may change). A service worker adds a cache-first strategy for media assets, falling back to the network. Profile images use a versioned URL pattern (/avatars/{userId}?v={hash}) so that cache invalidation happens naturally when the hash changes.

Feed ordering cache is the trickiest aspect. The ordered list of post IDs for the "Top" sort is cached with a 5-minute TTL because the ranking algorithm's output changes as engagement accrues. The "Latest" sort cache has a 1-minute TTL since new posts appear frequently. Cache invalidation for specific entities happens via the real-time WebSocket — when an engagement_update event arrives, the corresponding entity in the cache is updated in-place. For prefetching, when the user is 3 items from the bottom of the current page, the next page is pre-fetched and cached, creating a seamless infinite scroll experience with no visible loading states.`,

    performance: `The most impactful performance optimization is feed virtualization. Only items within the viewport plus a 3-item overscan buffer in each direction are mounted in the DOM. As the user scrolls, items leaving the viewport are unmounted and their DOM nodes are recycled. This keeps the DOM node count under 100 even with thousands of items in the feed. Each item's height is measured once on first render and cached in a height map for accurate scroll position calculation.

Image performance uses responsive loading via srcset and sizes attributes, with blur-up placeholders generated server-side (tiny base64-encoded thumbnails embedded in the API response). Images below the fold use loading="lazy" for native browser lazy loading, combined with an IntersectionObserver-based preload that starts fetching images 2 viewport heights before they become visible. Video content uses poster frames and only initializes the video player (heavy JS) when the user interacts or the video enters the viewport.

React rendering is optimized through strategic memoization. FeedItem is wrapped in React.memo with a shallow comparison of the post entity and engagement counts. PostBody uses useMemo for text truncation and link parsing since these are computationally expensive. The reaction animation uses CSS transforms and opacity (GPU-composited properties) rather than layout-triggering properties. Large feed updates (batch insertion of 20 items) use startTransition to mark them as non-urgent, preventing input lag during rapid scrolling. Code splitting ensures the feed module loads independently, and below-fold features (comment section, share modal, report dialog) are dynamically imported.`,

    security: `Content injection is the primary security concern in a feed system. All user-generated content (post text, comments, usernames) is sanitized server-side using a whitelist-based HTML sanitizer (e.g., DOMPurify configuration that allows only safe formatting tags). The client additionally uses React's JSX escaping for text content and dangerouslySetInnerHTML only for server-sanitized rich text, with a CSP nonce attribute. Embedded links in post content are parsed and validated — they display the actual URL domain regardless of the anchor text to prevent phishing, and external links use rel="noopener noreferrer" with an interstitial warning page.

Authentication tokens are transmitted via HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite=Strict cookies — never in URL parameters or localStorage. The WebSocket connection authenticates via a short-lived token exchanged during the initial handshake, not the session cookie. API endpoints enforce authorization checks — a user can only see feed items permitted by the author's privacy settings and their relationship to the author.

Media content hosted on CDN uses signed URLs with short expiration (15 minutes) to prevent unauthorized direct access. Uploaded media is scanned for malware and CSAM before being served in the feed. Rate limiting on interaction endpoints (likes, comments) prevents spam — 100 reactions per minute, 20 comments per minute per user. The report feature allows users to flag content, and reported content is quarantined from the reporter's feed immediately while awaiting moderation review.`,

    accessibility: `The feed implements a landmark-based page structure with role="feed" on the main feed container, providing semantic meaning for assistive technologies. Each feed item uses article elements with aria-labelledby pointing to the post author and timestamp, giving screen reader users immediate context. The feed supports aria-setsize and aria-posinset attributes on each article (estimated total items and current position) to communicate list position.

Keyboard navigation allows Tab to move between interactive elements (like buttons, comment buttons, overflow menus) within each feed item, and a custom keyboard shortcut (J/K keys, opt-in) enables item-level navigation similar to email clients. The "New posts available" banner is announced via aria-live="polite" and is keyboard-focusable. Infinite scroll has a keyboard-accessible alternative — a "Load more" button that appears when the user reaches the end of loaded content via Tab navigation, since IntersectionObserver-based loading may not trigger for keyboard-only users.

Media content includes comprehensive alt text (generated via AI alt-text service on upload), and videos include captions/transcripts. The reaction picker is a fully accessible radio group with aria-label descriptions for each reaction type. Time-relative labels ("2 hours ago") include a datetime attribute with the full ISO timestamp in a time element for programmatic access. All interactive elements have visible focus indicators (outline: 2px solid with sufficient contrast), and the feed respects prefers-reduced-motion by disabling scroll animations, reaction animations, and video autoplay.`,

    errorHandling: `Feed error handling distinguishes between feed-level errors (entire feed fails to load), item-level errors (a single item fails to render), and interaction-level errors (a like or comment fails). Feed-level errors display a full-page error state with a descriptive message and retry button, while preserving any cached content below the error banner. The error state categorizes failures: "Network error — check your connection" for fetch failures, "Something went wrong" for 5xx errors, and "Feed not available" for 404s.

Item-level errors are handled by an ErrorBoundary wrapping each FeedItem. If a single item throws during rendering (e.g., corrupted data, missing required fields), the ErrorBoundary catches it and renders a collapsed placeholder ("This post couldn't be displayed") without crashing the entire feed. The error is logged to the monitoring service with the item ID and error details for debugging.

Interaction errors use the optimistic rollback pattern. When a like request fails, the local state reverts the like count and reaction state, and a toast notification appears: "Couldn't save your reaction. Try again." For comment creation failures, the failed comment remains in the UI with a red error indicator and a "Retry" button, preserving the user's typed content. For critical failures (auth errors during interaction), a modal prompts re-authentication. The real-time WebSocket connection uses reconnection with exponential backoff (1s, 2s, 4s, 8s, max 30s) and falls back to polling GET /api/feed/updates every 30 seconds after 5 failed reconnection attempts.`,

    offlineStrategy: `The offline strategy centers on IndexedDB as the primary offline storage, with a service worker managing network requests. When the user loads the feed while online, the service worker caches the API response and stores normalized entities in IndexedDB. On subsequent visits while offline, the service worker serves the cached API response, and the application reads entities from IndexedDB, rendering the last-known feed state instantly.

Offline interactions are queued in an IndexedDB "outbox" table. When the user likes a post, comments, or bookmarks while offline, the optimistic update is applied locally and the server request is stored in the outbox with a timestamp and retry count. When connectivity resumes (detected via navigator.onLine + a connectivity check endpoint), a SyncManager processes the outbox queue in order, applying actions to the server. Conflicts are resolved with a last-write-wins strategy for likes/reactions and a server-merge strategy for comments (both the offline comment and any server-side comments are preserved).

Media content uses a tiered offline strategy. Text content and low-resolution image placeholders are always cached. Full-resolution images for the first 20 feed items are cached during idle time using requestIdleCallback. Video content is never cached offline due to storage constraints. The service worker implements a stale-while-revalidate strategy for the feed API — cached content is shown immediately while a background fetch updates the cache. A "Last updated X minutes ago" indicator in the feed header communicates the staleness of offline content to the user.`,

    scalability: `At 10x scale (10x more content items per feed), the virtualization strategy becomes essential rather than optional. The virtual list implementation must handle variable-height items efficiently, maintaining a running height offset table that enables O(log n) scroll-to-index operations. The entity store must support pagination of entities — not all 10,000 items' entity data can be kept in memory. An eviction policy unloads entity data for items more than 50 viewport heights away from the current scroll position, re-fetching when they scroll back into range.

At 100x scale (100x more users creating content, higher update frequency), the real-time system must throttle updates. Instead of individual engagement update events, the server sends batched delta updates every 10 seconds: "Post A: +15 likes, +3 comments; Post B: +2 shares". The client applies these deltas without re-fetching full entity data. The WebSocket connection may need to be replaced with a more efficient protocol like gRPC-Web for binary-encoded event streams, reducing bandwidth.

For global scale, the feed must be edge-cached. The initial feed page (for logged-out users or the "Trending" feed) is rendered at the CDN edge using edge-side SSR, reducing time-to-first-byte to under 100ms globally. Personalized feeds use a two-phase load: the shell (header, sidebar, composer) is served from the edge cache, and the personalized content is fetched from the origin via a subsequent API call. Media assets are served from regional CDN PoPs with automatic format negotiation (AVIF for Chrome, WebP for Safari, JPEG for legacy). The feed's JavaScript bundle is split by route and feature, with critical feed rendering code (< 50KB) inlined and the rest loaded asynchronously.`,

    tradeoffs: `The choice between cursor-based and offset-based pagination is foundational. Cursor-based pagination prevents duplicate items when new content is inserted (a problem with offset-based: inserting item at position 0 shifts all offsets), but cursors are opaque and don't support "jump to page N" functionality. Cursor encoding can also leak implementation details if not properly encrypted. The tradeoff favors cursors for infinite scroll feeds where jumping to arbitrary positions isn't needed.

Optimistic UI versus confirmed UI is a spectrum. Full optimistic updates (instantly showing the like, then rolling back on failure) provide the best UX but risk showing incorrect state. Confirmed updates (showing a loading spinner until the server responds) are always consistent but feel slow. The hybrid approach (optimistic for low-risk actions like likes, confirmed for high-risk actions like delete/report) adds complexity in distinguishing action types but provides the best user experience while maintaining data integrity.

Virtualization introduces the tradeoff between scroll performance and feature richness. A virtualized list unmounts off-screen items, which means features like "find in page" (Ctrl+F) won't search off-screen content, and anchor links to specific posts won't work without custom scroll-to-item logic. Content that relies on being in the DOM (like ongoing video playback, active animations) gets disrupted when unmounted. The alternative of keeping all items in the DOM with CSS-based hiding preserves these features but fails at scale. The chosen approach uses virtualization with compensation — scroll-to-item via API, and pinning the currently-playing video item to prevent unmounting.`,
  },
  {
    id: "sd-chat",
    title: "Chat Application",
    difficulty: "Advanced",
    category: "System Design",
    tags: [
      "real-time",
      "websocket",
      "messaging",
      "typing-indicators",
      "presence",
      "encryption",
    ],
    requirements: `The chat application must support real-time one-on-one and group messaging with sub-second message delivery. Users must be able to send text messages, emoji reactions, file attachments (images, documents, audio), and reply to specific messages (threaded replies). The system must display typing indicators showing when other participants are composing messages, and read receipts showing which messages have been seen by each participant.

The conversation list must show all active conversations sorted by most recent activity, with unread message counts, the last message preview, and the sender's online/offline status. Group conversations must support up to 100 participants, with admin controls for adding/removing members, changing the group name and avatar, and muting notifications. The search functionality must allow users to search through message history within a conversation and across all conversations.

Message delivery must be reliable with at-least-once delivery guarantees. Messages sent while offline must be queued and delivered when connectivity resumes. The system must display message status indicators: sending (clock icon), sent (single checkmark), delivered (double checkmark), and read (blue double checkmark). The application must support push notifications for new messages when the app is in the background or the tab is inactive.`,

    constraints: `Messages must appear in the recipient's conversation within 500ms of sending under normal network conditions. The typing indicator must update within 200ms of the user's keystroke. The conversation list must load within 1 second, displaying the 20 most recent conversations. Message history pagination should load 50 messages per page with sub-second response times.

The application must handle at least 100 concurrent conversations per user and 10,000 messages per conversation in history without performance degradation. File attachments are limited to 25MB per file. The WebSocket connection must automatically reconnect within 5 seconds of disconnection, with no message loss during the reconnection window.

Client-side message storage in IndexedDB must support up to 500MB of data per user. The initial JavaScript bundle for the chat module must not exceed 200KB gzipped. The UI must maintain 60fps animations during message sending, receiving, and scrolling. The system must function as a progressive web app, working in standalone mode with offline capabilities.`,

    highLevelArchitecture: `The chat application architecture is built around a persistent WebSocket connection as the primary communication channel, with REST API fallbacks for non-real-time operations. The frontend is divided into three main subsystems: the ConversationManager (handles conversation list, creation, and metadata), the MessageManager (handles message sending, receiving, rendering, and history), and the PresenceManager (handles online status, typing indicators, and read receipts).

The WebSocket layer uses a custom protocol with typed message frames: { type: string, payload: unknown, id: string, timestamp: number, ack?: boolean }. Outbound messages include a client-generated UUID for idempotency and optimistic rendering. The server acknowledges each message with an ack frame containing the server-assigned message ID and timestamp. The client maintains a send queue that buffers messages during disconnection and replays them upon reconnection, using the client UUID for deduplication on the server side.

The data layer uses IndexedDB as the source of truth for message history, with an in-memory cache (Zustand store) for the current conversation's messages and the conversation list. When the app loads, it reads conversation metadata from IndexedDB, renders the conversation list, then establishes the WebSocket connection and fetches any missed messages since the last known server timestamp. A SyncEngine coordinates between the WebSocket event stream, the REST API, and IndexedDB to maintain consistency across the three data layers. The NotificationManager interfaces with the Notifications API and a service worker for background push notifications.`,

    componentArchitecture: `The root component is ChatApp, which manages the two-pane layout: ConversationListPane on the left and MessagePane on the right (or a single pane on mobile with navigation). ChatApp is wrapped in ChatProvider (WebSocket connection, current user, active conversation state) and NotificationProvider (notification permissions, sound settings).

ConversationListPane contains SearchBar, ConversationFilter (All, Unread, Groups, Archived), and ConversationList. ConversationList renders ConversationItem components, each showing Avatar (with online indicator), ConversationInfo (name, last message preview, timestamp), and UnreadBadge. ConversationItem is memoized by conversation ID and updatedAt timestamp.

MessagePane contains ConversationHeader (group info, call buttons, search toggle), MessageList (virtualized scrollable area), TypingIndicator (animated dots with participant names), and MessageComposer. MessageList renders MessageBubble components grouped by MessageDateSeparator. Each MessageBubble contains MessageContent (text, media, or file), MessageReply (quoted parent message), MessageReactions (emoji reaction pills), and MessageStatus (checkmarks). The MessageComposer contains TextInput (with auto-resize, emoji picker trigger, and mention autocomplete), AttachmentButton (file picker with drag-drop zone), and SendButton. The emoji picker is a lazily-loaded panel component using dynamic import since it contains a large emoji dataset.`,

    stateManagement: `State is split into three domains with different persistence strategies. Transient UI state (which conversation is active, composer text, emoji picker open, scroll position) uses React local state and refs — this state is cheap to reconstruct and doesn't need persistence. Session state (WebSocket connection status, user presence map, typing indicators) uses a Zustand store that resets on page load. Persistent state (conversations, messages, user profiles) uses IndexedDB as the primary store, with a Zustand-powered in-memory cache for the active conversation.

The message state for the active conversation follows the pattern: { messages: Message[], pendingMessages: PendingMessage[], hasOlderMessages: boolean, oldestLoadedTimestamp: number }. PendingMessage extends Message with additional fields: { clientId: string, status: "sending" | "failed", retryCount: number }. When a message is sent, it's added to pendingMessages with status "sending" and rendered in the UI immediately. When the server ack arrives, the message is moved from pendingMessages to messages with the server-assigned ID and timestamp.

The conversation list state uses a normalized map plus an ordered ID array: { conversationMap: Record<string, Conversation>, orderedIds: string[], unreadCounts: Record<string, number> }. When a new message arrives for a non-active conversation, only unreadCounts[conversationId] is incremented and the conversation is moved to the top of orderedIds — the full message data is written to IndexedDB without loading it into memory. Typing indicators use a dedicated ephemeral store: { typingUsers: Record<string, { userId: string, conversationId: string, expiresAt: number }> } with a 3-second auto-expiry timer that clears stale indicators if the "stopped typing" event is lost.`,

    apiDesign: `The REST API handles CRUD operations and history fetching. GET /api/conversations?limit=20&cursor={cursor} returns paginated conversations with embedded last-message previews. GET /api/conversations/{id}/messages?before={timestamp}&limit=50 returns paginated message history using timestamp-based cursor (not offset) for consistency during concurrent writes. POST /api/conversations creates a new conversation: { type: "direct" | "group", participantIds: string[], name?: string }. PUT /api/conversations/{id} updates metadata. POST /api/messages/upload handles file attachment uploads with multipart/form-data, returning a media URL used in the subsequent message.

The WebSocket protocol defines these client-to-server frame types: message.send (new message with clientId), message.typing (typing start/stop), message.read (mark messages as read up to a timestamp), presence.update (online/away/offline status). Server-to-client frame types: message.new (incoming message from another user), message.ack (confirmation of sent message with serverId mapping), message.status (delivery/read status updates), message.typing (other user typing), presence.change (user status change), conversation.update (metadata changes, member added/removed).

Each WebSocket frame includes a monotonically increasing sequence number for ordering. The client tracks the last received sequence number and includes it in reconnection handshakes — the server replays any events with higher sequence numbers, ensuring gap-free delivery. The heartbeat mechanism sends ping frames every 30 seconds, with the server responding with pong frames including the current server timestamp for clock synchronization.`,

    caching: `Message caching uses IndexedDB with a per-conversation object store, indexed by timestamp for efficient range queries (loading messages between two timestamps for pagination). Messages are written to IndexedDB as they arrive via WebSocket, creating a local replica of the conversation history. When loading a conversation, the app first reads from IndexedDB and renders immediately, then fetches any newer messages from the server using the latest local timestamp as the cursor.

Conversation metadata is cached in IndexedDB with a separate "sync" store tracking the last sync timestamp per conversation. On app load, a lightweight GET /api/conversations/updates?since={lastSync} returns only conversations modified since the last sync, minimizing the bootstrap payload. User profile data (names, avatars) is cached in a separate IndexedDB store with a 1-hour TTL and versioned avatar URLs for cache-friendly updates.

Media caching uses the Cache API via a service worker. Received image and file thumbnails are cached permanently (they're immutable content-addressed URLs). Full-resolution images are cached with an LRU eviction policy capped at 200MB. The service worker intercepts media fetches and serves from cache first, falling back to network. For conversation-specific emoji (custom stickers), a pre-fetch downloads the sticker pack when the user opens a conversation that uses custom stickers, caching them for offline use.`,

    performance: `Message list performance relies on virtualization with a reverse-scroll pattern. The list renders from bottom to top, with new messages appended at the bottom. Only messages within the viewport plus a 10-message overscan buffer are mounted. When loading older history (scrolling up), new messages are prepended without shifting the scroll position — this requires careful scroll position preservation by measuring content height before and after DOM updates and adjusting scrollTop accordingly.

Message rendering is optimized through memoization at multiple levels. MessageBubble is memoized by message ID and reactions array reference. Text content parsing (URLs, mentions, markdown) uses useMemo with the message text as the dependency. Emoji rendering uses a sprite sheet rather than individual image loads. Large emoji datasets for the picker are lazy-loaded and code-split into their own chunk.

WebSocket message processing is batched during high-throughput scenarios (e.g., joining a group with recent activity). Instead of dispatching individual state updates for each incoming message, the WebSocket handler buffers messages for 16ms (one frame) and dispatches a batch update using React's automatic batching. This prevents render thrashing when 50 messages arrive simultaneously. The conversation list uses CSS containment and content-visibility: auto to prevent off-screen conversation items from triggering layout calculations during updates.`,

    security: `End-to-end encryption (E2EE) for direct messages uses the Signal Protocol (Double Ratchet Algorithm). Each user generates an identity key pair and pre-key bundles on registration, uploaded to the server. When initiating a conversation, the client fetches the recipient's pre-key bundle and establishes a shared secret using X3DH (Extended Triple Diffie-Hellman). Messages are encrypted with AES-256-GCM using ratcheted keys. The server stores only encrypted ciphertext and cannot read message content. Group messages use a group key distributed via pairwise E2EE channels.

Authentication uses short-lived JWT access tokens (15-minute expiry) stored in memory (not localStorage) with httpOnly refresh tokens in secure cookies. The WebSocket connection authenticates via a one-time token exchanged through the REST API — the client requests a WebSocket ticket from POST /api/auth/ws-ticket, receiving a single-use token valid for 30 seconds, which is included in the WebSocket URL. This prevents WebSocket hijacking via URL sharing.

File attachments are scanned for malware server-side before delivery. Images are stripped of EXIF metadata (which may contain GPS coordinates) before storage. Content Security Policy headers prevent inline script execution. The client implements certificate pinning for the WebSocket connection on mobile apps. Message retention policies allow users to set auto-delete timers (24 hours, 7 days, 30 days) for conversations, with the client securely wiping IndexedDB entries upon expiration using overwrite-then-delete to prevent forensic recovery.`,

    accessibility: `The chat interface implements a live-region-based architecture for real-time content. Incoming messages are announced via an aria-live="polite" region that reads the sender name and message preview. Typing indicators use aria-live="polite" with aria-atomic="true" to announce "Alice is typing" without repeating for each keystroke update. The message list uses role="log" which signals to screen readers that new content is appended chronologically.

Keyboard navigation supports Tab through conversation list items, Enter to open a conversation, Escape to return to the conversation list. Within a conversation, Tab moves to the message composer, Shift+Tab moves to the message list for history navigation. Arrow keys navigate between messages in the list, and Enter on a message opens a context menu with Reply, React, Copy, and Delete options. The emoji picker is a grid with arrow key navigation and type-ahead search.

Message status indicators use aria-label descriptions: "Sending", "Sent", "Delivered", "Read by 3 participants". Emoji reactions include aria-label with the reaction name and count: "laughing face reaction, 5 people". File attachments include descriptive labels with file name, type, and size. The two-pane layout adapts for screen magnification — at 200% zoom, it switches to a single-pane navigation pattern. All custom components (toggles, dropdowns, popovers) follow WAI-ARIA widget patterns with proper roles, states, and keyboard interaction.`,

    errorHandling: `Message sending failures are handled with automatic retry and clear user feedback. When a message fails to send (network error or server error), it remains in the message list with a red error indicator and a "Failed to send — Tap to retry" label. The system automatically retries failed messages up to 3 times with exponential backoff (2s, 4s, 8s). If all retries fail, the message stays in the "failed" state until the user manually taps retry or deletes it. Failed messages are persisted in IndexedDB so they survive app restarts.

WebSocket disconnection is handled transparently. When the connection drops, a subtle banner appears: "Reconnecting..." with an animated indicator. The client implements a reconnection strategy with jittered exponential backoff (initial 1s, max 30s, with random jitter to prevent thundering herd). During disconnection, outbound messages are queued in memory and persisted to IndexedDB. On reconnection, the client sends its last-received sequence number, and the server replays missed events before processing the queued outbound messages.

Media upload failures use a chunked upload strategy where each chunk is individually retryable. If a chunk fails, only that chunk is retried (not the entire file). Progress state is preserved — a 90% uploaded file resumes from the last successful chunk. For unrecoverable errors (file too large, unsupported format), clear error messages are shown with actionable guidance. The application uses a global ErrorBoundary that catches rendering errors and shows a "Chat encountered an error — Refresh to continue" message while preserving the chat history in IndexedDB for recovery.`,

    offlineStrategy: `The offline chat experience maintains full read access and limited write capabilities. When offline, users can browse all cached conversations, read message history stored in IndexedDB, and view previously loaded media. The conversation list, message history, and user profiles are all available from IndexedDB, providing a rich offline reading experience with no loading spinners.

Outbound messages composed while offline are stored in an IndexedDB "outbox" with status "queued". They appear in the message list with a queued indicator (cloud with arrow icon). When connectivity resumes, the SyncEngine processes the outbox in chronological order, sending each message via the re-established WebSocket connection. The server handles idempotency via the client-generated UUID — if a queued message was partially processed before disconnection, the server detects the duplicate and returns the existing message rather than creating a duplicate.

The service worker implements a background sync registration using the Background Sync API. When the device regains connectivity (even if the app is closed), the service worker wakes up and processes queued messages. Push notifications continue to be received via the service worker even when the app is closed — the service worker stores incoming messages in IndexedDB and shows a system notification. When the user opens the app from the notification, the message is already in the local store and renders immediately. The offline indicator in the chat header shows "Offline — messages will be sent when you're back online" and transitions to "Syncing..." when connectivity returns.`,

    scalability: `At 10x scale (10x more messages per conversation), the IndexedDB storage and message list virtualization must handle much larger datasets. Message search becomes critical — a client-side full-text search index (using a library like FlexSearch) is maintained in a Web Worker, updated incrementally as messages arrive. The search index is bounded to the last 50,000 messages per conversation to prevent memory bloat, with older messages searched via the server API.

At 100x scale (10x more conversations, 10x more participants in groups), the conversation list itself needs virtualization. The WebSocket event volume increases significantly — in a group with 100 members, typing indicators alone could generate hundreds of events per second. The client must aggregate typing events: rather than showing "Alice, Bob, Charlie, David, Eve are typing...", it shows "5 people are typing..." when the count exceeds 3. Presence updates are batched by the server into 10-second windows.

For massive scale, the architecture shifts toward a pub/sub model where the WebSocket connection subscribes only to active conversations. Conversations the user hasn't opened in 24 hours are unsubscribed, and their updates are delivered via push notification or fetched lazily when opened. Message storage transitions from full local replication to on-demand fetching with aggressive caching — only the last 100 messages per conversation are stored locally, with older messages fetched from the server. The encryption key management for E2EE at scale requires a key server with proper key rotation and multi-device synchronization via a key distribution protocol.`,

    tradeoffs: `The choice between WebSocket and Server-Sent Events (SSE) + REST involves several tradeoffs. WebSocket provides full-duplex communication, enabling efficient typing indicators and presence updates without separate HTTP requests. However, WebSocket connections are harder to load-balance (sticky sessions required), harder to debug (not visible in browser network tab like HTTP requests), and don't benefit from HTTP/2 multiplexing. SSE + REST is simpler to implement and debug but doubles the connection count and adds latency for client-to-server events. The WebSocket choice is justified by the high-frequency bidirectional communication pattern of chat.

Local-first vs server-first architecture is a fundamental tradeoff. The local-first approach (IndexedDB as source of truth, synced to server) provides instant message rendering and full offline support but requires complex conflict resolution, data migration strategies when the schema changes, and careful storage quota management. The server-first approach (server as source of truth, local cache for performance) simplifies consistency but degrades the offline experience and adds latency to every interaction. The hybrid approach chosen here — IndexedDB as a local replica with server reconciliation — balances both but requires a SyncEngine with significant complexity.

End-to-end encryption provides strong privacy guarantees but severely limits server-side functionality. With E2EE, the server cannot index messages for search (search must happen client-side), cannot moderate content proactively, cannot generate message previews for push notifications (only "You have a new message"), and cannot migrate message history to new devices without the user's key backup. The tradeoff between privacy and functionality is managed by making E2EE opt-in per conversation, with clear UX indicators showing which conversations are encrypted.`,
  },
  {
    id: "sd-file-upload",
    title: "File Upload System",
    difficulty: "Advanced",
    category: "System Design",
    tags: [
      "chunked-upload",
      "progress-tracking",
      "retry",
      "cancellation",
      "drag-drop",
      "resumable",
    ],
    requirements: `The file upload system must support single and multiple file uploads with drag-and-drop, file picker, and paste-from-clipboard input methods. It must handle files up to 5GB in size using chunked uploading, with real-time progress tracking per file and overall progress for batch uploads. Each upload must be individually pausable, resumable, and cancellable without affecting other uploads in the batch.

The system must validate files before upload: checking file type against an allowlist (configurable per context), file size limits, file name length and character restrictions, and optionally image dimensions and aspect ratios. Validation must provide clear, actionable error messages. After upload, the system should display a preview — image thumbnails, video previews, document icons with file type indicators, and audio waveform previews.

The upload UI must show per-file status including file name, size, progress percentage with progress bar, upload speed, estimated time remaining, and action buttons (pause/resume, cancel, retry). Failed uploads must clearly indicate the failure reason and offer one-click retry. The system must handle duplicate detection — if a file with the same hash has already been uploaded, the server should return the existing file reference without re-uploading (deduplication).`,

    constraints: `Chunked upload uses 5MB chunks by default, with adaptive chunk sizing based on connection speed (1MB for slow connections, 10MB for fast). Maximum concurrent chunk uploads per file is 3 (parallel chunk upload), and maximum concurrent file uploads is 3. These limits prevent overwhelming the network and browser memory.

The system must support resumable uploads — if the browser crashes or the user navigates away and returns, uploads resume from the last successfully uploaded chunk rather than restarting. This requires persisting upload state (file reference, chunk map, upload URL) in IndexedDB. The upload must work in Web Workers to prevent blocking the main thread during chunk preparation (hashing, slicing).

Memory usage must stay below 200MB even when uploading multiple large files simultaneously — this means chunks must be streamed from the file using Blob.slice() rather than reading the entire file into memory. The drag-and-drop zone must work across the entire page (not just a small target), and must handle directory drops with recursive file extraction. The system must support upload to both direct server endpoints and cloud storage pre-signed URLs (S3, GCS).`,

    highLevelArchitecture: `The architecture is organized into four layers: the UI layer (drag-drop zone, file list, progress indicators), the upload orchestrator (manages upload queue, concurrency, and lifecycle), the chunk manager (handles individual chunk preparation and transmission), and the persistence layer (IndexedDB for resume state, background sync for offline queuing).

The data flow begins when files are added through any input method. The UI layer passes File objects to the UploadOrchestrator, which creates an UploadTask for each file. The UploadTask first runs through the ValidationPipeline (type check, size check, dimension check), then through the DeduplicationCheck (computes a file hash and queries the server). If the file is a duplicate, the task completes immediately with the existing file URL. Otherwise, the UploadTask enters the upload queue.

The UploadOrchestrator processes the queue respecting concurrency limits. For each active upload, it creates a ChunkManager that slices the file into chunks using Blob.slice() (zero-copy, no memory allocation), computes a checksum for each chunk, and uploads chunks in parallel (up to 3 concurrent). The ChunkManager reports per-chunk progress to the UploadTask, which aggregates it into file-level progress. A PresignedURLManager handles obtaining and refreshing upload URLs for cloud storage targets. The entire upload lifecycle (task creation, chunk progress, completion, failure) is persisted to IndexedDB by a PersistenceManager, enabling resume after page reload.`,

    componentArchitecture: `The component tree starts with UploadProvider (context for upload state and configuration) wrapping the feature area. The primary input component is DropZone, which wraps a target area and handles dragover, dragenter, dragleave, and drop events with visual feedback (border highlight, overlay message). Inside DropZone, a FileInput (hidden input[type="file"] triggered by a button click) and PasteHandler (listens for paste events on the document) provide alternative input methods.

The upload progress UI is rendered by UploadPanel, a fixed-position or sidebar component containing UploadHeader (overall progress, total files, total size), UploadList (scrollable list of UploadItem components), and UploadFooter (batch actions: cancel all, retry failed). Each UploadItem renders FileThumbnail (image preview or file type icon), FileInfo (name, size, speed, ETA), ProgressBar (segmented progress showing individual chunk states), and ActionButtons (pause/resume, cancel, retry).

ProgressBar is a detailed component showing chunk-level progress — each chunk is a segment of the bar, colored differently based on state: gray (pending), blue (uploading), green (completed), red (failed). This gives users granular visibility into the upload's internal state. When a file completes, UploadItem transitions to a CompletedItem variant showing the file preview with a copy-link button and delete button. An ErrorItem variant shows the error message with a retry button and a details expandable section.`,

    stateManagement: `Upload state is managed by a Zustand store with the following shape: { uploads: Record<string, UploadState>, queue: string[], activeUploads: string[], completedUploads: string[], failedUploads: string[], config: UploadConfig }. Each UploadState contains: { id: string, file: { name, size, type, lastModified }, status: "validating" | "hashing" | "queued" | "uploading" | "paused" | "completed" | "failed" | "cancelled", progress: { loaded: number, total: number, speed: number, eta: number }, chunks: ChunkState[], uploadUrl: string, resultUrl: string, error: Error | null, createdAt: number }.

ChunkState tracks individual chunk progress: { index: number, offset: number, size: number, status: "pending" | "uploading" | "completed" | "failed", checksum: string, attempts: number }. This granular state enables the segmented progress bar and precise resume — on resume, only chunks not in "completed" status are re-uploaded.

The store uses immer for immutable updates with mutable syntax, which is essential for the frequent nested updates (updating a specific chunk's progress within a specific upload). File objects themselves are NOT stored in the Zustand store (they're not serializable) — instead, they're held in a WeakMap keyed by upload ID, accessible to the ChunkManager but not part of the reactive state. Speed calculation uses a sliding window of the last 5 seconds of transfer data to smooth out fluctuations. The persistence middleware serializes the upload state (minus file blobs) to IndexedDB every 2 seconds and on status changes.`,

    apiDesign: `The upload flow uses a three-phase API. Phase 1 — initiation: POST /api/uploads/init with { fileName: string, fileSize: number, mimeType: string, checksum: string, chunkSize: number, chunkCount: number, metadata?: Record<string, string> }. The response returns { uploadId: string, uploadUrl: string, chunks: Array<{ index: number, presignedUrl: string, expiresAt: string }> } for pre-signed URL uploads, or { uploadId: string } for direct server uploads. If the checksum matches an existing file, the response includes { duplicate: true, fileUrl: string }, short-circuiting the upload.

Phase 2 — chunk upload: PUT {presignedUrl} with the chunk blob and Content-MD5 header for integrity verification. For direct server uploads: PUT /api/uploads/{uploadId}/chunks/{chunkIndex} with the chunk blob. Each chunk upload returns { success: boolean, chunkIndex: number, receivedSize: number, checksum: string }. The server validates the chunk checksum against the Content-MD5 header and returns 409 if mismatched.

Phase 3 — completion: POST /api/uploads/{uploadId}/complete with { chunks: Array<{ index: number, etag: string }> }. The server assembles chunks, verifies the complete file checksum, performs virus scanning, and returns { fileUrl: string, fileId: string, metadata: { size, mimeType, dimensions?, duration? } }. A separate endpoint GET /api/uploads/{uploadId}/status returns current upload state for resume scenarios. DELETE /api/uploads/{uploadId} cancels an in-progress upload and cleans up server-side chunks.`,

    caching: `The caching strategy focuses on three areas: deduplication caching, upload state persistence, and preview caching. For deduplication, file checksums (computed via the Web Crypto API's SHA-256 in a Web Worker) are cached in IndexedDB keyed by a composite of file name, size, and last-modified timestamp. This avoids re-hashing unchanged files. The server maintains a checksum-to-file mapping, and the client sends the checksum during upload initiation to detect duplicates before any chunk transfer occurs.

Upload state persistence uses IndexedDB as described in the state management section. When the user returns to the page, the PersistenceManager loads incomplete upload states from IndexedDB. For each incomplete upload, it verifies that the local file is still accessible (the File API reference may be invalid after a browser restart — in this case, the user is prompted to re-select the file) and queries the server for the upload's current chunk status via GET /api/uploads/{uploadId}/status. The system reconciles local and server state, resuming from the furthest confirmed chunk.

Preview caching generates and stores image thumbnails client-side using OffscreenCanvas in a Web Worker. Thumbnails are stored in an IndexedDB "previews" store, keyed by file checksum, with a 100MB storage cap and LRU eviction. For subsequent uploads of the same file, the thumbnail is loaded from cache instantly. Pre-signed URLs for chunk uploads are cached locally with expiry awareness — if a URL expires before the chunk is uploaded, the system re-requests a fresh URL from the initiation endpoint.`,

    performance: `The primary performance optimization is parallel chunk uploading. Each file's chunks are uploaded through 3 concurrent HTTP requests, maximizing network throughput. Chunk size adapts dynamically — the system measures the upload speed of the first few chunks and adjusts the chunk size to target 2-5 second upload times per chunk (small enough for frequent progress updates, large enough to minimize HTTP overhead). The adaptive algorithm uses: optimalChunkSize = measureBandwidth * 3 seconds, clamped to [1MB, 10MB].

File hashing for deduplication and integrity runs in a Web Worker using the streaming SHA-256 API (crypto.subtle.digest). The file is read in 2MB slices and fed to the hash incrementally, preventing memory spikes for large files. Thumbnail generation for image previews also runs in a Web Worker using OffscreenCanvas, decoding the image at reduced resolution and encoding a JPEG thumbnail.

The drag-and-drop handler processes dropped items asynchronously. For directory drops (using the DataTransferItem.webkitGetAsEntry() API), the recursive directory traversal runs in batches of 100 files to prevent blocking. Progress updates to the UI are throttled to 60fps using requestAnimationFrame — the ChunkManager reports progress at the byte level, but the store update and re-render happens at most once per frame. The UploadList is virtualized for batch uploads exceeding 20 files. Upload speed calculation uses a weighted moving average over the last 10 seconds to provide smooth, non-jumpy ETA estimates.`,

    security: `File validation is the first security layer. The system validates MIME types by reading the file's magic bytes (first 8 bytes via FileReader), not relying solely on the file extension or the browser-reported type. This prevents uploading executable files disguised with image extensions. The server performs independent validation of the uploaded content, including virus scanning via ClamAV integration, and rejects files that fail validation even if they pass client-side checks.

Upload authentication uses short-lived pre-signed URLs for cloud storage targets. Each chunk's pre-signed URL expires in 15 minutes and is scoped to the specific chunk index and upload ID. The initiation endpoint requires a valid session token and enforces per-user upload quotas (total storage, daily upload volume, concurrent uploads). CSRF protection uses the standard token-in-header approach.

Content integrity is verified at multiple levels: client computes Content-MD5 for each chunk, the server verifies it on receipt, and the final assembly step verifies the complete file checksum against the initial declaration. This prevents both corruption and tampering. For sensitive uploads, the client can encrypt chunks with AES-256-GCM before upload using a user-provided key, with the encryption running in a Web Worker. Uploaded files are served from a separate domain (CDN) with Content-Disposition: attachment headers to prevent stored XSS through uploaded HTML/SVG files.`,

    accessibility: `The drag-and-drop zone is fully accessible to non-mouse users. The DropZone renders a visually prominent "Choose files" button that triggers the hidden file input on click/Enter. The drop zone itself has role="region" and aria-label="File upload area. Drag files here or use the choose files button." It does not rely solely on drag-and-drop — the button provides equivalent functionality for keyboard and assistive technology users.

Upload progress is communicated through multiple channels. The ProgressBar has role="progressbar" with aria-valuenow, aria-valuemin, and aria-valuemax attributes that update as chunks complete. An aria-live="polite" region announces significant status changes: "file.jpg upload started", "file.jpg 50% complete", "file.jpg upload finished", "file.jpg upload failed — server error". These announcements are throttled to 25% intervals to avoid overwhelming screen reader users with constant updates.

Error states are clearly communicated with aria-invalid and aria-errormessage for validation errors during file selection. The retry button includes aria-label="Retry upload for file.jpg". The cancel confirmation dialog is a proper dialog role with aria-modal and focus trapping. File type icons include aria-label descriptions ("PDF document", "JPEG image"). Keyboard shortcuts include Delete to remove a completed upload and Space to pause/resume an active upload when the upload item is focused. All interactive elements have visible focus indicators with sufficient contrast.`,

    errorHandling: `Chunk-level error handling enables granular retry without restarting entire uploads. When a chunk upload fails, the ChunkManager categorizes the error: network errors (no response) trigger automatic retry with exponential backoff (1s, 2s, 4s, max 3 retries); 5xx server errors use the same retry strategy; 4xx client errors (except 408 Request Timeout, which is retried) are treated as permanent failures. When a chunk exhausts its retries, the file upload transitions to "failed" status, but other files in the batch continue unaffected.

File-level errors are categorized and presented with actionable messages. "File too large" errors include the size limit. "Unsupported file type" errors list the allowed types. "Storage quota exceeded" errors suggest deleting old files. "Network error" provides a retry button. "Server error" provides both retry and a "Contact support" link. The error state preserves all upload progress — when the user clicks retry after a transient error, the upload resumes from the last completed chunk.

Pre-signed URL expiration is handled proactively. The ChunkManager checks URL expiry timestamps before each chunk upload. If a URL is expired or will expire within 30 seconds, it requests fresh URLs from the server before proceeding. If the refresh request itself fails, the upload is paused (not failed) with a message indicating temporary unavailability. Global error handling includes a circuit breaker that pauses all uploads after 10 consecutive failures across any upload, displaying "Upload service appears to be unavailable — uploads will resume automatically when the service recovers" and checking the health endpoint every 30 seconds.`,

    offlineStrategy: `When the user goes offline during an upload, all active chunk transfers are paused immediately (XHR/fetch requests are aborted to release network resources). The upload state transitions to "paused" with a sub-status of "offline". The UploadPanel shows a banner: "Uploads paused — waiting for connection." Queued uploads remain in the queue, and completed uploads' results are preserved.

Upload state is persisted to IndexedDB with enough information to resume: the upload ID, server-assigned upload URL, chunk map with per-chunk status and checksums, file metadata, and the original file name (for re-selection if the File handle is lost). The File System Access API (where available) is used to maintain persistent file handles that survive page reloads, avoiding the need for users to re-select files. On browsers without this API, users are prompted to re-select the file via a file picker that validates the file matches the expected name, size, and checksum.

When connectivity resumes, the system waits 2 seconds for stable connectivity (avoiding premature resume on flaky connections), then queries the server for each paused upload's status. If the server has expired the upload (uploads expire after 24 hours of inactivity), the system re-initiates the upload but sends the file checksum for deduplication — if the server already has partial chunks from a previous upload with the same checksum, it can transfer ownership, avoiding re-upload of completed chunks. The service worker registers for background sync, enabling upload resumption even if the page isn't open when connectivity returns.`,

    scalability: `At 10x file count (uploading 100+ files in a single batch), the upload queue management becomes critical. The UploadOrchestrator implements a priority queue with configurable strategies: size-ascending (small files first for quick wins), size-descending (large files first to start heavy transfers early), or FIFO (upload order). The UploadList must virtualize to handle 100+ items without DOM bloat. Batch operations (cancel all, retry all failed) use optimized state updates that modify multiple entries in a single store transaction.

At 10x file size (50GB files), chunk management must handle 5,000-10,000 chunks per file. The chunk map is stored as a typed array (Uint8Array where each byte represents a chunk's status) rather than an array of objects, reducing memory overhead from megabytes to kilobytes. The deduplication hash computation for a 50GB file takes significant time — the system shows a "Preparing file..." progress bar during hashing and allows the user to skip dedup and start uploading immediately (hashing continues in the background, and if a duplicate is detected mid-upload, the remaining chunks are skipped).

At 100x throughput (many users uploading simultaneously), the architecture must consider server-side implications. Client-side adaptations include: dynamic concurrency reduction when the server responds with 429 (Too Many Requests) or response times exceed thresholds; adaptive chunk sizing that reduces chunk size during congestion; and a client-side token bucket rate limiter that spreads upload requests evenly over time rather than bursting. The pre-signed URL approach offloads bandwidth from the application server to cloud storage, scaling more efficiently than proxying uploads through the app server.`,

    tradeoffs: `Chunked vs streaming upload is the first architectural decision. Chunked uploads (slicing file into discrete pieces) provide pause/resume capability, per-chunk retry, and progress granularity at the cost of higher request overhead (HTTP headers per chunk) and server-side complexity (chunk tracking, assembly). The Streams API enables true streaming upload (fetch with ReadableStream body) with lower overhead but provides no pause/resume capability and limited retry granularity (the entire stream must restart on failure). For files under 10MB, single-request upload is simpler and faster. The system uses adaptive behavior: single request for files < 10MB, chunked for files > 10MB.

Client-side hashing for deduplication adds upload latency — hashing a 1GB file takes 2-5 seconds on modern hardware. This delay frustrates users who want to see immediate upload progress. The tradeoff is latency vs bandwidth savings. For contexts where deduplication savings are high (backup systems, collaboration tools with shared files), the hashing delay is justified. For contexts where duplicates are rare (photo uploads, document submissions), hashing can be skipped or deferred. The system makes hashing opt-in at the configuration level.

Web Worker usage for hash computation and thumbnail generation adds complexity (message passing, blob transfer via structured clone or transferable objects) but keeps the main thread responsive. Without Workers, a 5-second hash computation would freeze the UI entirely. The complexity tradeoff is especially significant for the file resume scenario — the Worker must be re-initialized on page reload, and the file data must be re-transferred to it. The system uses a WorkerPool with 2 Workers, balancing parallelism (hash + thumbnail simultaneously) with memory overhead.`,
  },
  {
    id: "sd-analytics",
    title: "Analytics Dashboard",
    difficulty: "Advanced",
    category: "System Design",
    tags: [
      "charts",
      "real-time-data",
      "filters",
      "data-aggregation",
      "performance",
      "visualization",
    ],
    requirements: `The analytics dashboard must display multiple data visualizations including line charts (time series), bar charts (categorical comparisons), pie/donut charts (proportions), area charts (cumulative metrics), tables with sorting and pagination, and KPI cards with sparklines. The dashboard must support a configurable date range picker with presets (today, last 7 days, last 30 days, custom range) that updates all visualizations simultaneously.

Users must be able to apply global filters (by region, product category, user segment, traffic source) that propagate to all dashboard widgets. Each widget should also support widget-level filters for drill-down analysis. The dashboard layout must be configurable — users should be able to rearrange widgets via drag-and-drop, resize them, add new widgets from a catalog, and save custom dashboard layouts. Dashboard state (layout, filters, date range) must be serializable as a URL for sharing.

Real-time data updates must be supported for key metrics — active users, revenue, error rates — with smooth animations during data transitions. The system must support data export (CSV, PDF) for individual widgets and the entire dashboard. A comparison mode should allow overlaying two time periods (e.g., this week vs last week) on the same chart. The dashboard should support alerts/thresholds — visual indicators when a metric exceeds or drops below a configured threshold.`,

    constraints: `Dashboard initial load must complete within 3 seconds, rendering above-the-fold widgets with data. Below-the-fold widgets can load progressively. Each widget's data fetch must complete within 2 seconds. The dashboard must handle datasets of up to 100,000 data points per chart without performance degradation.

The system must work on screens ranging from 1024px to 3840px wide, with a responsive grid layout. Charts must be interactive (hover tooltips, click-to-drill-down) while maintaining 60fps during interactions. The total JavaScript bundle for the dashboard module should not exceed 300KB gzipped (charting libraries are the major cost).

Data freshness requirements vary by metric: real-time metrics update every 5 seconds, hourly metrics refresh every minute, and daily metrics refresh every 5 minutes. The dashboard must support up to 20 widgets simultaneously without memory issues. PDF export must render charts server-side to maintain visual fidelity regardless of the user's screen size.`,

    highLevelArchitecture: `The dashboard architecture follows a widget-based composable pattern with a central state coordinator. The top level consists of DashboardShell (layout framework, header, filters), WidgetGrid (responsive grid with drag-drop), and DataCoordinator (centralized data fetching and distribution). Each widget is an independent unit with its own data requirements, rendering logic, and error handling, but shares global context (date range, filters) via the DataCoordinator.

Data flows through a query layer that normalizes widget data requirements into API queries. When global filters change, the DataCoordinator invalidates affected widget queries and re-fetches data in parallel. The query layer uses a request deduplication strategy — if two widgets need the same underlying metric (e.g., "revenue by day" for both a line chart and a KPI card), a single API request serves both. The caching layer (React Query or a custom solution) manages request lifecycle, caching, and background refetching.

Real-time data flows through a dedicated WebSocket channel that pushes metric snapshots every 5 seconds. The DataCoordinator merges real-time updates with cached historical data, creating a seamless transition from historical to live data. The export pipeline runs separately — when a user requests PDF export, the dashboard serializes its current state (layout, data, filters) and sends it to a server-side rendering service that uses Puppeteer to render the dashboard into a PDF with consistent styling.`,

    componentArchitecture: `The root component is DashboardPage, containing DashboardHeader (title, date range picker, global filter bar, export button, share button), WidgetGrid (the main content area), and DashboardConfigPanel (slide-out panel for adding/configuring widgets). DashboardPage is wrapped in DashboardProvider (shared dashboard state) and QueryProvider (data fetching context).

WidgetGrid uses a library like react-grid-layout to render a responsive, draggable, resizable grid. Each grid cell contains a Widget wrapper component that provides WidgetHeader (title, widget-level filters, overflow menu with export/remove/configure), WidgetBody (the visualization), WidgetFooter (last updated timestamp, refresh button), and WidgetErrorBoundary. The Widget wrapper handles loading, error, and empty states generically.

Visualization components are specialized: LineChart, BarChart, PieChart, AreaChart, DataTable, KPICard, and SparklineCard. Each accepts a standardized DataSeries interface: { labels: string[], datasets: Array<{ name: string, values: number[], color: string }>, metadata: { unit: string, format: string } }. This abstraction allows any widget to switch between chart types. The DateRangePicker component is a compound component with CalendarGrid, PresetButtons, ComparisonToggle, and ApplyButton. The FilterBar renders FilterChip components for active filters and a FilterModal for adding filters.`,

    stateManagement: `Dashboard state is divided into three categories. Configuration state (layout, widget configs, saved preferences) is persisted to the backend and synced with URL query parameters. This state changes infrequently and uses a Zustand store with persistence middleware. The shape is: { layout: GridLayout[], widgets: Record<string, WidgetConfig>, dateRange: { start: Date, end: Date, preset: string | null, comparison?: { start: Date, end: Date } }, globalFilters: Filter[], savedDashboardId: string | null }.

Data state (fetched metrics, chart data) is managed by React Query (TanStack Query), which provides caching, background refetching, and stale-while-revalidate semantics. Each widget's data has a unique query key derived from its widget type, date range, and filters: ["widget", widgetId, dateRange, filters]. When global filters change, all widget queries are invalidated and refetched in parallel. React Query's structural sharing prevents unnecessary re-renders when refetched data is identical.

UI interaction state (which widget is being dragged, which filter modal is open, tooltip position) uses local component state. Real-time data uses a separate Zustand slice: { realtimeMetrics: Record<string, number>, lastUpdated: Record<string, number>, isConnected: boolean }. The WebSocket handler updates this store, and widgets that subscribe to real-time metrics re-render independently. URL serialization captures dateRange, globalFilters, and layout hash, enabling dashboard sharing via URL. The URL is updated using replaceState (not pushState) to avoid polluting browser history with every filter change.`,

    apiDesign: `The analytics API uses a flexible query language. The primary endpoint is POST /api/analytics/query with a body: { metrics: string[] (e.g., ["revenue", "active_users", "conversion_rate"]), dimensions: string[] (e.g., ["date", "region", "product_category"]), filters: Array<{ field: string, operator: "eq" | "ne" | "gt" | "lt" | "in" | "between", value: unknown }>, dateRange: { start: string, end: string }, granularity: "minute" | "hour" | "day" | "week" | "month", limit?: number, offset?: number }. The response shape is: { data: Array<Record<string, unknown>>, meta: { totalRows: number, executionTimeMs: number, truncated: boolean }, columns: Array<{ name: string, type: "number" | "string" | "date", format?: string }> }.

This query-based API replaces per-widget endpoints. The line chart widget issues a query with metrics=["revenue"], dimensions=["date"], granularity="day". The pie chart issues metrics=["revenue"], dimensions=["product_category"]. The table widget uses multiple dimensions with pagination. This approach allows arbitrary widget configurations without backend changes.

A separate endpoint GET /api/analytics/metadata returns available metrics, dimensions, and their valid filter operators — this powers the filter UI's autocomplete and validation. The real-time endpoint is GET /api/analytics/realtime?metrics=active_users,revenue,error_rate which returns current values and is called via SSE (Server-Sent Events) for push updates. The export endpoint POST /api/analytics/export accepts the full dashboard state (queries, layout) and returns a job ID. GET /api/analytics/export/{jobId} polls the export status and returns the download URL when complete.`,

    caching: `Data caching operates at both the query level and the rendering level. Query-level caching uses React Query with staleness configuration per metric type: real-time metrics stale after 5 seconds, hourly metrics after 1 minute, daily metrics after 5 minutes. Background refetching keeps data fresh without blocking the UI. Query deduplication ensures that identical queries triggered by multiple widgets result in a single network request.

The rendering cache stores computed chart data (labels, datasets, scales) in useMemo within each widget. When raw data hasn't changed but the user resizes a widget, the chart doesn't recompute its data transformation — only the SVG/Canvas rendering scales. For expensive data transformations (aggregations, moving averages, percentile calculations), a Web Worker compute pipeline processes raw data and returns chart-ready datasets, which are cached by input hash.

Dashboard-level caching stores the last successful data snapshot in sessionStorage, enabling instant rendering when the user navigates away and returns. The snapshot includes a timestamp, and if the data is still within the staleness window, it's used directly without an API call. Pre-fetching is used for common navigation patterns — when the user hovers over a date range preset, the system pre-fetches data for that range. Filter prediction also pre-fetches: if the current view is "All regions" and the filter dropdown is opened, data for each individual region is pre-fetched in the background.`,

    performance: `Chart rendering performance is the primary concern with 100,000+ data points. The system uses canvas-based rendering (not SVG) for large datasets — Chart.js or a custom Canvas rendering pipeline. For time-series data with more data points than pixels, the system applies downsampling using the Largest-Triangle-Three-Buckets algorithm, reducing 100,000 points to the number of horizontal pixels while preserving visual peaks and valleys. Tooltip interaction uses a spatial index (binary search on sorted x-values) rather than iterating all points.

Dashboard load performance uses a progressive loading strategy. Above-the-fold widgets (determined by the saved layout) fetch data with priority. Below-the-fold widgets use IntersectionObserver to trigger data fetching only when they scroll into view. The widget skeleton (card with header, correct size placeholder) renders immediately from the cached layout configuration, providing visual stability. Charts animate from zero using requestAnimationFrame with easing, creating a smooth reveal effect.

Bundle size is managed through careful dependency selection. A full charting library (Chart.js ~60KB, D3 ~80KB) is loaded only when the dashboard module is accessed (route-based code splitting). Individual chart types are further split — the PieChart component dynamically imports only the pie chart module, not the entire library. Grid layout library code is loaded only when the dashboard is in edit mode (drag-drop enabled). The DataTable uses virtual rows for tables exceeding 100 rows, keeping DOM node count constant regardless of data size.`,

    security: `Analytics data is sensitive — it reveals business performance and user behavior. The API enforces role-based access control: viewers see pre-configured dashboards, editors can create/modify dashboards and widgets, and admins can access raw data export and configure alerts. The query API validates all metric and dimension requests against the user's permitted data scope — a regional manager can only query data for their region.

Dashboard sharing via URL uses encrypted state tokens rather than plain query parameters to prevent data leak through URL sharing. The share URL format is /dashboard?state={encrypted_token}, where the token is encrypted with a dashboard-specific key and includes an expiry timestamp. Accessing a shared dashboard requires authentication, and the server validates the requesting user's permissions against the dashboard's access control list.

Export functionality applies data masking rules — PII fields are hashed or redacted in exports based on the user's data access level. PDF exports include a watermark with the exporter's identity and timestamp for audit compliance. The real-time SSE endpoint validates the authentication token per-event (not just at connection time) to handle token expiration during long-lived connections. All API responses include Cache-Control: private, no-store headers to prevent sensitive analytics data from being cached by intermediary proxies.`,

    accessibility: `Charts implement a multi-modal accessibility strategy. Each chart includes a visually hidden data table equivalent accessible via a "View as table" toggle button. The table provides the same information in a screen-reader-accessible format with sortable columns. Chart legends use role="list" with interactive legend items that can be toggled via keyboard (Space to show/hide a dataset, Tab to navigate between legend items).

Color choices meet WCAG contrast requirements and use patterns/textures in addition to color to differentiate datasets (important for color-blind users). The prefers-contrast media query is respected — in high-contrast mode, chart lines use thicker strokes and data points use larger markers. All chart colors are tested against common color blindness types (deuteranopia, protanopia, tritanopia) and use a colorblind-safe palette by default.

The dashboard grid is navigable via keyboard — Tab moves between widgets, and each widget's interactive elements (filter buttons, chart interactions, export) are reachable via Tab within the widget. Drag-and-drop widget rearrangement has a keyboard alternative: a "Move widget" button opens a dialog with position selection via arrow keys. Date range and filter controls use standard form patterns with label associations, error descriptions, and constrained input. Live-updating real-time values use aria-live="polite" with aria-atomic="true" to announce new values without overwhelming the screen reader with constant updates (throttled to once per 30 seconds for real-time metrics).`,

    errorHandling: `Widget-level error isolation ensures that one failing widget doesn't break the entire dashboard. Each widget's WidgetErrorBoundary catches rendering errors and displays a compact error state: "Unable to load this widget" with a Retry button and a Details expandable showing the error message. The error state preserves the widget's size in the grid to prevent layout shifts. Common errors have specific messages: "Date range too large — try a shorter period" for 413 responses, "No data available for these filters" for empty results, "Query timed out — try fewer metrics or a shorter range" for 504 responses.

Data fetching errors use React Query's retry and error handling. Transient errors (network failures, 5xx) retry 3 times with exponential backoff. Persistent errors transition the widget to an error state. The DataCoordinator detects correlated failures — if 80%+ of widget queries fail simultaneously, it shows a dashboard-level error banner ("Dashboard data service is experiencing issues") rather than individual widget errors, reducing visual noise.

Real-time data disconnection is handled gracefully. When the SSE connection drops, real-time widgets show a "Live updates paused" indicator and display the last known value with a "last updated 30 seconds ago" timestamp. The system attempts reconnection with backoff and automatically resumes live updates. If the reconnection fails for more than 5 minutes, widgets switch to polling mode (fetching every 30 seconds via REST) as a degraded-but-functional fallback. Export failures (PDF generation timeout, CSV too large) show a toast with the specific issue and suggest alternatives (shorter date range, fewer widgets, different export format).`,

    offlineStrategy: `The dashboard implements a read-only offline mode that serves the most recently viewed dashboard state. When the dashboard loads successfully, its data is stored in IndexedDB keyed by dashboard ID, date range, and filter combination. The service worker intercepts API requests and, when offline, returns the cached data with a "Served from cache" header that the app detects and displays as a "Viewing cached data from {timestamp}" banner.

Chart rendering works fully offline since it's client-side — the cached data is passed to the charting library, and all interactions (hover tooltips, legend toggles) remain functional. Filter changes while offline search the cached data — if data for the requested filter combination was previously fetched and cached, it's served. If not, the widget shows "Data not available offline for these filters." Date range changes are similarly limited to cached ranges.

The service worker pre-caches the dashboard shell (HTML, CSS, JS) using a cache-first strategy, ensuring the dashboard UI loads instantly even on flaky connections. For teams that rely on dashboards in areas with poor connectivity (warehouses, field offices), an explicit "Save for offline" feature downloads expanded date ranges and additional filter combinations into IndexedDB, creating a comprehensive offline snapshot. Real-time widgets obviously cannot function offline — they show the last known value with a clear "Live data unavailable" state and resume automatically when connectivity returns.`,

    scalability: `At 10x widget count (200 widgets across multiple dashboard tabs), the rendering strategy must change. Only the active tab's widgets are mounted — inactive tabs unmount their widget components but retain their data in the React Query cache. The widget grid uses CSS contain: layout paint to isolate rendering. The DataCoordinator implements request priority queuing — visible widgets get high-priority fetches, and prefetched data for other tabs uses low-priority requests with requestIdleCallback.

At 100x data volume (10 million data points per query), server-side aggregation becomes mandatory. The API must perform rollups at appropriate granularity — daily data for year-long ranges, hourly for month ranges, minute-level for day ranges. The client specifies desired granularity, and the server uses pre-computed aggregation tables (materialized views) rather than scanning raw data. Client-side downsampling is still needed as a safety net, but the server should never send more than 10,000 points per query.

At organizational scale (1000 users viewing the same dashboard), the real-time data pipeline must handle fan-out efficiently. The SSE/WebSocket connection from each client connects to a pub/sub system (Redis Pub/Sub or NATS) rather than querying the database. Dashboard state sharing uses a CDN — when a user shares a dashboard URL, the encrypted state token is stored on a CDN-backed key-value store with edge caching, enabling instant dashboard loads globally. Pre-rendered dashboard snapshots (server-side rendered HTML with embedded chart images) can be served for frequently accessed dashboards, eliminating the need for client-side chart rendering entirely for initial view.`,

    tradeoffs: `SVG vs Canvas for chart rendering is a critical decision. SVG provides DOM-based accessibility (each data point is a DOM element that can receive ARIA attributes and events), easier CSS styling, and crisp rendering at any zoom level. However, SVG performance degrades significantly beyond 5,000 elements. Canvas renders unlimited data points efficiently but loses individual element accessibility, requires custom hit-testing for interactions, and produces blurry output on high-DPI displays without explicit pixel ratio handling. The system uses SVG for small datasets (< 1,000 points) and Canvas for large datasets, with a data table alternative for accessibility in both cases.

Query granularity presents a freshness-vs-performance tradeoff. Fine-grained queries (per-minute data) provide detailed insights but generate massive payloads and slow rendering. Coarse-grained queries (per-day data) load fast but hide intraday patterns. The dashboard uses adaptive granularity — zooming into a chart section automatically increases granularity for that range, similar to how mapping applications load detail on zoom. This adaptive approach adds complexity in the data layer but provides the best user experience.

Dashboard sharing via URL state vs saved server-side state involves tradeoffs in functionality and complexity. URL state is shareable without authentication (the state is self-contained) but limited in size (URLs beyond 2KB may be truncated), and exposes filter parameters in browser history. Server-side saved state supports unlimited complexity and access control but requires authentication and a database table. The system uses both — compact states are URL-encoded, and large states are saved server-side with a short URL reference.`,
  },
  {
    id: "sd-notifications",
    title: "Notification System",
    difficulty: "Advanced",
    category: "System Design",
    tags: [
      "push-notifications",
      "in-app",
      "real-time",
      "priority",
      "preferences",
      "service-worker",
    ],
    requirements: `The notification system must support multiple notification channels: in-app notifications (bell icon with dropdown panel), browser push notifications (via the Push API and service worker), email notifications (triggered by backend events), and mobile push notifications (via platform-specific services). Each channel must be independently configurable per notification type — users should be able to receive "new comment" notifications in-app and via push but not email, while receiving "security alert" notifications on all channels.

In-app notifications must display in a scrollable panel accessible via a bell icon in the header, with an unread count badge. Each notification shows an icon (typed by notification category), a title, a description, a relative timestamp, and an action button (e.g., "View comment", "Accept invite"). Notifications must be grouped by category (social, system, security, activity) with section headers. Mark-as-read must work both individually (clicking a notification) and in bulk (mark all as read button).

The system must support notification priority levels: critical (security alerts, payment failures — always shown, with sound), high (direct messages, mentions — shown immediately), normal (comments, likes — batched), and low (weekly digests, recommendations — queued for quiet periods). Do-not-disturb mode should suppress all non-critical notifications. The system must maintain a complete notification history with infinite scroll, search, and filtering by type, read status, and date range.`,

    constraints: `In-app notifications must appear within 2 seconds of the triggering event. The notification panel must load and render within 500ms of clicking the bell icon. The notification list must support 10,000+ historical notifications with smooth scrolling (virtualization required). Unread count updates must appear within 1 second of a new notification arrival.

Push notification delivery must succeed within 10 seconds for high-priority notifications. The service worker push handler must process and display notifications within 100ms of receipt. Notification payloads must stay under 4KB per message (Push API limitation). The notification system's JavaScript footprint must not exceed 30KB gzipped to avoid impacting the host application's performance.

The system must handle high notification throughput — a user who is mentioned in a viral post might receive hundreds of notifications in minutes. Batching, rate limiting, and intelligent aggregation ("Alice and 47 others liked your post") are essential. The notification preferences page must be responsive and work offline (loading from cached preferences).`,

    highLevelArchitecture: `The notification architecture has three main subsystems: the delivery pipeline, the in-app notification UI, and the preference engine. The delivery pipeline receives events from the backend via a WebSocket connection (or SSE fallback) and routes them through the preference engine to determine which channels should fire. The in-app UI manages the notification panel, bell icon badge, and notification history. The preference engine evaluates user preferences, priority rules, and DND status to filter and route notifications.

The real-time connection receives raw notification events from the server: { id: string, type: NotificationType, priority: Priority, title: string, body: string, data: Record<string, unknown>, groupKey?: string, timestamp: number }. The NotificationRouter processes each event: it checks user preferences for the notification type, applies priority rules (critical bypasses DND, low is queued), evaluates aggregation rules (groupKey-based batching), and dispatches to the appropriate channels — in-app store, Push API, or both.

The in-app notification store is the source of truth for notification state. It maintains a paginated list of all notifications (fetched from GET /api/notifications on initial load, augmented by real-time events), tracks read/unread status, manages the unread count badge, and handles optimistic mark-as-read. The Push API channel uses a service worker that receives push events, formats them into browser notifications, and handles notification click events (opening the appropriate app page). A separate NotificationAnalytics module tracks delivery, impression, click, and dismiss events for measuring notification effectiveness.`,

    componentArchitecture: `The notification UI is anchored by NotificationBell in the app header — a button component showing a bell icon with a NotificationBadge (red circle with unread count, hidden when zero). Clicking the bell toggles the NotificationPanel, a fixed-position dropdown panel.

NotificationPanel contains NotificationTabs (All, Unread, categorized tabs), NotificationActions (Mark all read, Settings link), NotificationList (virtualized scrollable list), and EmptyState (friendly illustration when no notifications). NotificationList renders NotificationItem components grouped under NotificationGroupHeader (date-based: "Today", "Yesterday", "This Week"). Each NotificationItem contains NotificationIcon (category-specific icon with color), NotificationContent (title, body text, action link), NotificationTimestamp (relative time), and NotificationDot (unread indicator). Clicking a NotificationItem marks it as read and navigates to the relevant content.

The NotificationPreferences page is a separate route containing PreferenceSection components for each notification type. Each section has channel toggles (in-app, push, email) implemented as accessible switch components. A GlobalPreferences section manages DND schedule (quiet hours), notification sound toggle, and email digest frequency. The preference UI uses an optimistic update pattern — toggling a preference updates the local state immediately and syncs to the server in the background.`,

    stateManagement: `Notification state uses a dedicated Zustand store with the following shape: { notifications: Notification[], unreadCount: number, unreadByCategory: Record<string, number>, isLoading: boolean, hasMore: boolean, cursor: string | null, preferences: NotificationPreferences, dndActive: boolean, panelOpen: boolean, activeFilter: NotificationFilter }. The store is designed for high-frequency updates — new notifications arrive via WebSocket every few seconds.

The notifications array stores the most recent 100 notifications in memory for instant panel rendering. Older notifications are loaded on-demand via pagination. Each notification has: { id, type, priority, title, body, data, read: boolean, readAt: string | null, createdAt: string, groupKey: string | null, aggregatedCount: number }. Aggregated notifications collapse multiple events into one entry — "Alice and 47 others liked your post" has aggregatedCount: 48.

Unread count is maintained both in the store and via the WebSocket connection. When the app loads, it fetches the current count from GET /api/notifications/unread-count. Real-time events increment the count, and mark-as-read actions decrement it. To prevent count drift over long sessions, the system re-syncs the count every 5 minutes via a lightweight API call. Preferences are loaded once on app initialization, cached in the store and localStorage, and re-fetched when the user opens the preferences page. Preference changes trigger immediate updates to the routing logic — if a user disables push notifications for comments, pending comment notifications in the push queue are cancelled.`,

    apiDesign: `The REST API provides: GET /api/notifications?cursor={cursor}&limit=20&filter={type}&read={boolean} for paginated notification history. PATCH /api/notifications/{id}/read to mark a single notification as read. POST /api/notifications/mark-all-read to mark all notifications as read (optionally filtered by category). GET /api/notifications/unread-count returns { total: number, byCategory: Record<string, number> }. GET /api/notifications/preferences returns the user's notification preferences. PUT /api/notifications/preferences updates preferences.

The WebSocket channel pushes notification events: { type: "notification.new", payload: Notification } for new notifications, { type: "notification.aggregated", payload: { id: string, groupKey: string, newCount: number } } for aggregation updates, and { type: "notification.count", payload: { total: number } } for periodic count sync. The push notification subscription is managed via POST /api/notifications/push/subscribe { endpoint: string, keys: { p256dh: string, auth: string } } using the Web Push Protocol's PushSubscription object.

For notification aggregation, the server batches notifications with the same groupKey for 30 seconds before sending. During this window, individual events are accumulated and sent as a single aggregated notification. The client receives "Alice liked your post", then 30 seconds later receives an aggregated update: "Alice and 12 others liked your post", replacing the original individual notification. This prevents notification flooding during high-activity periods.`,

    caching: `Notification caching serves two purposes: instant panel rendering and offline access. The most recent 100 notifications are cached in IndexedDB with a dedicated object store, indexed by createdAt for chronological queries and by read status for unread filtering. When the notification panel opens, notifications are read from IndexedDB first, rendering instantly, while a background fetch updates the list from the server.

The unread count is cached in localStorage for instant badge rendering on page load — the badge shows the cached count immediately while the actual count is fetched from the API. This prevents the common anti-pattern of the badge appearing with a delay after page load. The service worker caches notification preference data for offline preference page rendering.

Push notification payloads are cached by the service worker in a dedicated cache for notification history. When the user clicks a push notification, the service worker stores the notification data in IndexedDB before opening the app URL. If the app is already open, the notification data is posted via BroadcastChannel to the running app instance. For notification grouping and aggregation, the cache maintains a groupKey-to-notificationId mapping, enabling efficient lookup when an aggregation update arrives (the client needs to find and update the existing grouped notification rather than adding a new one).`,

    performance: `The notification system is designed to have minimal impact on the host application. The notification module is code-split and loaded lazily — only the NotificationBell component (< 5KB) is included in the initial bundle. The full NotificationPanel and its dependencies are loaded on first click. The service worker for push notifications is registered after the main application has finished loading (using requestIdleCallback), preventing registration from competing with critical resource loading.

Notification list rendering uses virtualization for the panel (which has a fixed height) and intersection-observer-based pagination for the full history page. Each NotificationItem is memoized by notification ID and read status. The aggregation count update uses a targeted state update — when an aggregation event arrives, only the specific notification's count is updated, not the entire notifications array, preventing a full list re-render.

WebSocket message processing is efficient: the notification router uses a lookup table for preference checking (O(1) per notification type) rather than iterating through preference rules. Notifications arriving in burst (e.g., 50 likes in 1 second) are batched on the client side — the WebSocket handler buffers incoming notifications for 100ms and dispatches a batch update. The unread count badge uses CSS-only animations for the pulse effect on new notifications, avoiding JavaScript-driven animations that could cause jank.`,

    security: `Push notification security is critical because the push subscription contains an endpoint URL and encryption keys. The subscription object is stored server-side, associated with the user's account, and never exposed in API responses. The server uses VAPID (Voluntary Application Server Identification) for push messages, providing authentication that the push messages originate from the legitimate application server.

Notification content must be carefully sanitized. Since push notification titles and bodies can contain user-generated content (e.g., comment text), the server strips all HTML and limits content length before inclusion in the push payload. In-app notification rendering uses React's default JSX escaping for text content. Notification action URLs are validated against an allowlist of application routes — a malicious notification cannot redirect the user to an external phishing site.

Notification preferences are protected by authentication and rate-limited to prevent abuse. The DND schedule is enforced server-side, not just client-side, ensuring that even if the client-side check is bypassed, the server won't send push notifications during quiet hours (except for critical priority). The notification WebSocket connection uses the same authentication mechanism as the chat system — a one-time token exchanged via REST, valid for 30 seconds. Push notification encryption uses the Web Push Protocol's ECDH key agreement, ensuring that only the user's browser can decrypt the notification content.`,

    accessibility: `The NotificationBell button includes aria-label="Notifications" and aria-describedby referencing the unread count: "3 unread notifications". When the panel opens, focus moves to the first notification or the "no notifications" message. The panel is a dialog with aria-modal="false" (non-modal, allowing interaction with the rest of the page) and is dismissible via Escape.

Each NotificationItem is a button (or link) with aria-label combining the title, body, and timestamp: "New comment from Alice on your post, 5 minutes ago, unread". The unread indicator is communicated via aria-current or a visually hidden "(unread)" text. The "Mark all as read" button includes a confirmation for screen readers: "Marked 5 notifications as read" via an aria-live region after the action completes.

Push notifications use the Notification API's standard attributes for accessibility — the system provides title and body text that clearly describes the notification without relying on visual context. Notification grouping in the panel uses heading levels (h3 for date groups, h4 for category groups within dates) for screen reader navigation. The preferences page uses fieldset and legend for grouping channel toggles per notification type, making it clear which channels belong to which notification type. All toggle switches implement the WAI-ARIA switch pattern with proper state announcements.`,

    errorHandling: `Push notification subscription failures are handled gracefully. If the user denies notification permission, the system shows a persistent but dismissible banner: "Enable browser notifications to stay updated" with a link to instructions for re-enabling permissions. If the Push API subscription fails (service worker registration failure, push service unavailable), the system falls back to in-app-only notifications and logs the error for monitoring.

Real-time notification delivery failures trigger a fallback mechanism. If the WebSocket connection drops, the system switches to polling GET /api/notifications?since={lastTimestamp} every 30 seconds. If polling also fails, the unread count badge shows a warning indicator (orange dot instead of the count) indicating that the count may be stale. On reconnection, the system fetches all missed notifications since the last received timestamp and processes them in chronological order.

Individual notification action failures (mark as read, preference update) use optimistic rollback. If marking a notification as read fails server-side, the unread indicator reappears with a subtle animation, and the unread count is corrected. Preference save failures show an inline error next to the failed toggle: "Couldn't save this preference — retry" with the toggle reverted to its previous state. The service worker handles push event processing errors by catching exceptions and showing a generic "You have a new notification" fallback notification when the payload cannot be parsed.`,

    offlineStrategy: `Offline notification support focuses on preserving read access and queuing write actions. The IndexedDB store provides full access to cached notifications, including search and filtering on cached data. The notification preferences page loads from cached preferences in localStorage, allowing users to view (but not save) their settings offline. Changes made offline are queued in an IndexedDB "outbox" and synced when connectivity resumes.

The service worker handles push notifications independently of the app's online status. Push events arrive via the browser's push service regardless of connectivity (as long as the device has internet briefly), and the service worker displays them using the Notification API. The service worker stores received notification data in IndexedDB, making them available in the app's notification history when the user opens it. Click handling in the service worker uses the clients.openWindow() API to open the relevant app page.

For extended offline periods, the system implements a "catch-up" mechanism. When the app comes back online, it fetches all notifications since the last sync timestamp in a single paginated batch, processes them through the preference engine (to handle any preference changes made on other devices), and updates the local store. Notifications received via push during the offline period are deduplicated against this catch-up batch using notification IDs. The unread badge accurately reflects the count after catch-up is complete.`,

    scalability: `At 10x notification volume (hundreds of notifications per day per user), the aggregation system becomes essential. The server must aggregate similar notifications aggressively — instead of 50 individual "liked your post" notifications, one aggregated notification with the top 3 actor names and a count. The client-side notification list must handle frequent updates without re-rendering the entire list. Virtual list rendering with stable keys (notification ID) ensures that only new or updated items trigger rendering.

At 100x scale (organizational notifications — system alerts reaching thousands of users simultaneously), the WebSocket fanout architecture must handle broadcast efficiency. The server uses topic-based pub/sub — users subscribe to notification topics (their user ID, their team ID, global announcements), and the pub/sub system handles efficient message distribution. Client-side, the notification store implements a write buffer that batches incoming notifications into 200ms windows before updating the store, preventing render thrashing during notification storms.

For platforms with millions of users, push notification delivery requires a distributed push service that queues and rate-limits outbound push messages to prevent overwhelming browser push services (which may throttle or block abusive senders). The notification preferences system must support tenant-level defaults (organization admins set default preferences for all employees) with per-user overrides, requiring a preference resolution engine that merges multiple preference layers. Client-side, notification history pagination must use cursor-based loading with server-side filtering to handle users with 100,000+ historical notifications.`,

    tradeoffs: `Push notifications vs polling involves a fundamental tradeoff. Push via the Web Push API provides instant delivery, works when the tab is closed, and supports rich notifications (icons, actions, badges). However, it requires user permission (which many users decline — typical opt-in rates are 5-15%), depends on third-party push services (Google FCM, Apple APNs) with their own reliability characteristics, and has payload size limits (4KB). Polling via REST is universally supported, doesn't require permission, and has no payload limits, but adds latency (up to the polling interval), wastes bandwidth when there are no new notifications, and doesn't work when the tab is closed.

Notification aggregation timing is a key tradeoff. Shorter aggregation windows (5 seconds) deliver notifications quickly but result in many individual notifications during burst activity. Longer windows (60 seconds) produce cleaner aggregated notifications but delay delivery noticeably. The system uses adaptive aggregation — short windows during low activity (user gets individual notifications promptly) and longer windows during high activity (bursts are aggregated), with the server tracking per-user notification velocity to adjust the window dynamically.

Client-side vs server-side preference enforcement affects architecture complexity and reliability. Client-side enforcement (the client decides which channels to use) is simpler but unreliable — a buggy client might show suppressed notifications, and push notifications can't be easily suppressed client-side after they're sent. Server-side enforcement (the server applies preferences before dispatching to channels) is more reliable but requires the server to be aware of all client state (DND status, active tab). The system uses server-side enforcement for push and email channels (which the client can't control) and client-side enforcement for in-app notifications (which the client fully controls), combining both approaches.`,
  },
  {
    id: "sd-video",
    title: "Video Streaming Frontend",
    difficulty: "Senior",
    category: "System Design",
    tags: [
      "adaptive-streaming",
      "hls",
      "buffering",
      "video-controls",
      "fullscreen",
      "accessibility",
    ],
    requirements: `The video streaming frontend must support adaptive bitrate streaming (ABR) using HLS (HTTP Live Streaming) or DASH protocols, automatically adjusting video quality based on network conditions and device capabilities. The system must provide a full-featured player with custom controls: play/pause, seek bar with preview thumbnails, volume control with mute toggle, playback speed selector (0.5x to 2x), quality selector (auto + manual override), fullscreen toggle, picture-in-picture mode, and captions/subtitles toggle.

The player must support multiple content types: on-demand video (VOD) with full seek capability, live streaming with configurable latency (standard 10-30s or low-latency 2-5s), and live DVR (ability to seek back in a live stream's buffer). Content protection must be supported via DRM (Widevine for Chrome, FairPlay for Safari, PlayReady for Edge) for premium content. The system must display a video thumbnail/poster before playback, loading/buffering states, and a slate/overlay when content is unavailable.

Additional features include: a watch progress system (resume from where you left off), keyboard shortcuts for all controls, theater mode (wider player, darker surroundings), mini-player (floating small player during scroll), chapter markers on the seek bar, and end-screen recommendations. The player must report playback quality metrics (buffering events, quality switches, error rates) to an analytics service for Quality of Experience (QoE) monitoring.`,

    constraints: `The player must achieve < 2 second time-to-first-frame for VOD content and < 5 seconds for live streams. Rebuffering rate must stay below 1% of total watch time. The player UI must not block the first frame — controls overlay on the video after it starts playing. Quality switches must complete within 1 segment duration (typically 2-6 seconds) and be visually seamless.

The player JavaScript bundle must not exceed 100KB gzipped (excluding the HLS/DASH parser library, which is loaded on demand). The player must work on Chrome, Firefox, Safari, Edge (latest 2 versions), and mobile browsers (iOS Safari, Android Chrome). DRM content must work on all supported platforms, requiring platform-specific EME (Encrypted Media Extensions) implementation.

The player must handle seek operations within 500ms, including thumbnail preview generation. Memory usage must stay below 150MB even during long playback sessions (2+ hours), requiring proper segment eviction from SourceBuffer. The player must function behind corporate proxies and firewalls that may block WebSocket connections (fallback to HTTP-based protocols).`,

    highLevelArchitecture: `The architecture centers on a PlayerCore engine that orchestrates three subsystems: the StreamingEngine (ABR logic, segment fetching, buffer management), the UILayer (custom controls, overlays, interaction handling), and the AnalyticsEngine (QoE metrics collection and reporting). PlayerCore exposes a unified API that abstracts protocol differences (HLS vs DASH) and DRM variations.

The StreamingEngine uses either hls.js (for HLS in non-Safari browsers) or native HLS (Safari) or shaka-player (for DASH). It manages the MSE (Media Source Extensions) SourceBuffer, handles segment downloading with bandwidth estimation, implements ABR algorithms (throughput-based or buffer-based quality selection), and manages DRM license acquisition through the EME API. The engine operates as a state machine: IDLE → LOADING → BUFFERING → PLAYING → PAUSED → SEEKING → ENDED, with error states at each transition.

The data flow for video playback starts with the PlayerCore receiving a content manifest URL. The StreamingEngine fetches and parses the manifest (M3U8 for HLS, MPD for DASH), extracting available quality levels, segment URLs, DRM license server URLs, and subtitle tracks. The ABR controller selects an initial quality based on network speed estimation (using navigator.connection or measuring manifest fetch time). Segments are fetched, decrypted if needed (via EME), and appended to SourceBuffer. The UILayer renders controls over the video element, reading playback state via HTMLMediaElement events and MediaSource API. The AnalyticsEngine hooks into both the StreamingEngine (buffer health, quality switches) and UILayer (user interactions, play/pause events) to compile QoE reports.`,

    componentArchitecture: `The component tree starts with VideoPlayer as the root, containing VideoContainer (the video element with aspect ratio management), ControlsOverlay (conditionally visible controls), LoadingOverlay (spinner during buffering), ErrorOverlay (error state with retry), EndScreen (recommendations after playback ends), and MiniPlayer (floating player on scroll).

ControlsOverlay uses absolute positioning over the video and auto-hides after 3 seconds of inactivity (mouse movement resets the timer). It contains ProgressBar (seek bar with BufferIndicator, PlaybackPosition, ChapterMarkers, and ThumbnailPreview on hover), ControlsBar (left: PlayButton, VolumeSlider, TimeDisplay; center: empty or live indicator; right: SpeedSelector, QualitySelector, CaptionToggle, PIPButton, TheaterButton, FullscreenButton), and TopBar (title, back button for mobile, settings).

ProgressBar is the most complex component. It renders a multi-layer bar: the background track, a buffer-loaded indicator (light gray), the playback position indicator (red/blue), chapter markers (small dots or lines), and a hover preview layer showing a thumbnail sprite and timestamp. ThumbnailPreview uses a sprite sheet (a grid of small thumbnails at 1-second intervals, provided as a VTT file referencing a sprite image) and positions the correct sprite tile based on the hover position. QualitySelector renders available qualities with their resolution and bitrate, with the current quality highlighted and "Auto" as the default selection.`,

    stateManagement: `Player state is managed by a centralized PlayerStore (Zustand or a custom event-emitter-based store) that synchronizes with the HTMLMediaElement's native state. The store shape is: { playbackState: "idle" | "loading" | "buffering" | "playing" | "paused" | "seeking" | "ended" | "error", currentTime: number, duration: number, buffered: TimeRanges, volume: number, muted: boolean, playbackRate: number, quality: { current: QualityLevel, available: QualityLevel[], auto: boolean }, subtitles: { current: TextTrack | null, available: TextTrack[] }, fullscreen: boolean, pip: boolean, theater: boolean, error: PlayerError | null }.

The store subscribes to HTMLMediaElement events (timeupdate, progress, play, pause, seeking, seeked, ended, error, volumechange, ratechange) and mirrors the native state. This indirection is necessary because multiple components need to react to playback state changes, and directly querying the DOM element from each component would violate React's declarative paradigm. The currentTime update uses a throttled subscription (10 updates per second) to prevent excessive re-renders.

User preferences (volume, muted, playback speed, preferred quality, caption language) are persisted to localStorage and loaded on player initialization. Watch progress (current position and duration for each video) is stored in an IndexedDB "watch-history" store, debounce-saved every 10 seconds and on pause/close events. The mini-player state uses a separate context that tracks whether the user has scrolled past the main player and whether mini-player is enabled. Quality selection state distinguishes between auto mode (ABR algorithm decides) and manual mode (user locked a specific quality), persisting the preference per session.`,

    apiDesign: `The content API provides video metadata and streaming URLs. GET /api/videos/{id} returns { id, title, description, duration, poster: string (URL), manifest: { hls: string, dash: string }, drm: { widevine: { licenseUrl: string }, fairplay: { certificateUrl: string, licenseUrl: string } }, subtitles: Array<{ language: string, label: string, url: string }>, thumbnailSprite: { url: string, vtt: string }, chapters: Array<{ title: string, startTime: number }>, watchProgress?: { position: number, updatedAt: string } }.

DRM license acquisition uses protocol-specific endpoints. Widevine: POST {licenseUrl} with the EME-generated license request body, receiving the license response. FairPlay: GET {certificateUrl} for the server certificate, then POST {licenseUrl} with the SPC (Server Playback Context). These requests include the user's authentication token for entitlement verification — the DRM server checks that the user has access to the content before issuing a license.

Watch progress is saved via POST /api/videos/{id}/progress { position: number, duration: number, completedPercent: number }. This endpoint is called with debouncing (every 30 seconds) to avoid excessive server load. The analytics endpoint POST /api/analytics/playback receives batched events: { videoId, sessionId, events: Array<{ type: "play" | "pause" | "seek" | "buffer" | "quality_change" | "error", timestamp: number, data: Record<string, unknown> }> }. Events are batched client-side and sent every 30 seconds or on session end (using navigator.sendBeacon).`,

    caching: `Video segment caching is handled primarily by the browser's HTTP cache and the SourceBuffer. HLS/DASH segments use Cache-Control: max-age=31536000 with content-addressed URLs (the segment filename includes a hash), making them effectively immutable and permanently cacheable. The streaming engine's SourceBuffer acts as an in-memory cache of decoded video/audio frames — the engine manages buffer levels (maintaining 30 seconds of forward buffer for VOD, 10 seconds for live) and evicts old segments to stay within the browser's SourceBuffer quota.

Manifest caching differs between VOD and live content. VOD manifests are cached with long TTLs since they don't change. Live manifests are re-fetched at each segment interval (typically every 2-6 seconds) with Cache-Control: no-cache, as they contain the latest segment URLs. The service worker does NOT cache video segments (too large), but it caches the player UI assets (JS, CSS, sprites) with a cache-first strategy for instant player loading.

Thumbnail sprite sheets are cached aggressively in the HTTP cache (immutable content-addressed URLs) and pre-fetched after the manifest loads. Since sprite sheets can be 500KB-2MB, the system pre-fetches them at low priority using a Link: rel=prefetch header or requestIdleCallback-triggered fetch. Watch progress is cached in IndexedDB client-side, enabling instant "resume from where you left off" even before the server responds. DRM licenses are cached by the CDM (Content Decryption Module) for the license duration, enabling offline playback on supported platforms.`,

    performance: `Time-to-first-frame optimization uses multiple techniques. The manifest is fetched in parallel with player initialization — while the JS player library loads and initializes, the manifest URL is pre-fetched using a link rel=preload tag in the HTML head. The ABR algorithm starts with a conservative quality estimate (choosing a lower quality for faster first-segment download), then ramps up quality over the next 3-4 segments as bandwidth estimation stabilizes. The first segment is fetched from the nearest CDN edge server using anycast routing.

Buffer management directly impacts rebuffering rate and memory usage. The engine maintains a target forward buffer of 30 seconds for VOD (adjustable based on available memory) and 10 seconds for live. When the buffer exceeds 60 seconds (user paused for a long time), old segments are evicted from SourceBuffer to free memory. The ABR algorithm uses a two-pronged approach: a throughput estimator (EWMA of recent segment download speeds) for steady-state quality selection, and a buffer-based failsafe that drops quality aggressively when the buffer drops below 10 seconds to prevent rebuffering.

UI performance ensures controls don't jank during video playback. The controls overlay uses CSS contain: layout style paint and will-change: opacity for smooth show/hide animations. The seek bar's thumbnail preview positions are pre-calculated from the VTT file on load. The progress bar updates use requestAnimationFrame rather than timeupdate events (which fire at inconsistent rates). Video element rendering is offloaded to the GPU compositor layer via CSS transform: translateZ(0). The mini-player transition uses FLIP animation (First, Last, Invert, Play) for a smooth transition from the main player position to the mini-player position.`,

    security: `DRM implementation is the primary security concern. The player uses the EME (Encrypted Media Extensions) API to communicate with the browser's CDM (Content Decryption Module). License requests include a user authentication token and are sent over HTTPS to the DRM license server, which verifies entitlement before issuing a license. Licenses have configurable expiry — rental content uses 48-hour licenses, subscription content uses 30-day licenses, and live content uses per-session licenses.

Content URL protection prevents unauthorized direct access to video segments. Manifest and segment URLs use time-limited signed tokens (CDN token authentication) that expire after 6 hours, preventing URL sharing. The manifest URL is obtained from the content API (which requires authentication), and the token is embedded in segment URLs within the manifest. CDN edge servers validate the token before serving content.

The player implements anti-screen-capture measures for premium content: CSS overlay with -webkit-backface-visibility on the video container (prevents some screen capture tools), EME output protection settings (HDCP requirements), and watermarking via invisible overlay or video-level watermarks embedded in the stream. Player integrity is protected by obfuscating the player JavaScript and implementing runtime checks that verify the player is running in an expected environment (checking for known debugging tools or automation frameworks that might facilitate piracy).`,

    accessibility: `The custom video player implements the WAI-ARIA media player pattern. The player container has role="region" with aria-label="Video player: {title}". The play/pause button uses aria-label that changes based on state: "Play" or "Pause". The seek bar uses role="slider" with aria-valuemin="0", aria-valuemax="{duration}", aria-valuenow="{currentTime}", and aria-valuetext="2 minutes 30 seconds of 10 minutes 15 seconds". The volume slider similarly uses role="slider" with percentage-based aria-valuetext.

Keyboard controls follow platform conventions: Space/K for play/pause, Left/Right arrows for 5-second skip, Up/Down arrows for volume, M for mute, F for fullscreen, C for captions toggle, and numbers 0-9 for seeking to percentage positions (5 = 50%). All controls are reachable via Tab navigation with visible focus indicators. The control bar auto-hide behavior is suppressed when focus is within the controls (only mouse inactivity triggers auto-hide, not keyboard inactivity).

Captions support includes multiple subtitle formats (WebVTT, TTML), configurable caption appearance (font size, color, background, position) per WCAG and FCC requirements, and support for audio description tracks. The player provides text alternatives for all visual states: loading ("Video loading"), buffering ("Video buffering, please wait"), error states with descriptive messages, and quality change announcements via aria-live ("Video quality changed to 1080p"). The thumbnail preview on the seek bar includes aria-label with the timestamp for screen reader users hovering via touch exploration.`,

    errorHandling: `Playback error handling uses a categorized recovery strategy. Network errors during segment fetch trigger automatic retry with exponential backoff (500ms, 1s, 2s) and quality downgrade — if the current quality fails 3 times, the player drops to the next lower quality and retries. If all qualities fail, the player shows a user-facing error: "Playback error — check your connection" with a retry button that resets the streaming engine and attempts to resume from the last known good position.

DRM errors require specific handling. License acquisition failures retry twice, then show "This video is currently unavailable" (not exposing DRM details to the user). EME key session errors (expired license, revoked key) attempt a silent license renewal — if the renewal succeeds, playback continues uninterrupted. If renewal fails (subscription expired), the player shows an appropriate message: "Your subscription has expired — renew to continue watching."

Manifest parsing errors and codec incompatibilities are handled at initialization. If the HLS manifest references codecs not supported by the browser, the streaming engine filters to supported quality levels. If no supported quality exists, the player shows "This video format is not supported on your browser" with a suggestion to try a different browser. The analytics engine captures all error events with contextual data (browser, OS, network type, video ID, playback position, buffer health at time of error) for QoE monitoring and debugging. A circuit breaker pattern stops retry attempts after 5 consecutive failures within 30 seconds, showing a permanent error state to avoid battery drain on mobile devices.`,

    offlineStrategy: `Offline video playback is supported on platforms with persistent storage APIs (primarily mobile apps via Progressive Web App capabilities). The system uses the Cache API for downloaded content, with a DownloadManager that handles the download workflow: fetching the manifest, downloading all segments for a selected quality level, storing DRM licenses for offline use (using EME persistent license sessions), and caching subtitle files.

The download progress UI shows per-video download status: percentage complete, estimated size, download speed, and pause/cancel controls. Downloaded content appears in an "Offline" section of the UI, accessible without network connectivity. The service worker intercepts video segment requests for downloaded content and serves them from the Cache API, making the switch between online streaming and offline playback transparent to the player.

Storage management is critical for offline video. A single 2-hour movie at 720p uses approximately 1.5GB of storage. The system shows available storage before download and warns when storage is low. An automatic cleanup policy removes downloaded content after the viewing license expires or after a configurable retention period (30 days default). Partial downloads are resumable — the DownloadManager tracks which segments have been cached and picks up where it left off. For devices without sufficient storage or offline API support, the offline download option is hidden, and the player gracefully handles the case where a previously downloaded video's cache has been evicted by the browser.`,

    scalability: `At 10x concurrent viewers on a single video (viral content, live events), the CDN and origin architecture must handle the load. Frontend-side, the player does not change significantly — each viewer independently fetches segments from the CDN. However, the ABR algorithm should be more conservative during peak events, starting at lower quality and ramping up slowly, because CDN cache hit rates may be lower for recently encoded content. The analytics batching interval should increase (from 30s to 60s) to reduce analytics ingestion load.

At 100x content catalog (millions of videos), the player initialization must be efficient. Video metadata should be pre-fetched on hover (in a video listing page) so that clicking to play has zero metadata-fetch latency. The manifest URL can be included in the video card's data attributes, and a link rel=preload can be injected on hover. The thumbnail sprite system must scale — generating and storing sprite sheets for millions of videos is expensive. On-demand sprite generation (generating thumbnails from the video in real-time using server-side tools) with CDN caching reduces storage requirements at the cost of first-request latency.

For live streaming at scale (millions of concurrent viewers on a single stream), the architecture shifts to a multi-CDN strategy with a CDN load balancer that routes viewers to the optimal CDN based on geographic proximity and current CDN load. The player implements CDN failover — if segment fetches from the primary CDN fail, the player switches to an alternate CDN URL (provided in the manifest or via a separate CDN selection API). Edge-side manifest manipulation allows different CDN URLs to be injected per viewer, enabling granular traffic shaping.`,

    tradeoffs: `Native video element vs custom player is the foundational tradeoff. The native HTML5 video element with built-in controls is simple, accessible by default, and handles platform-specific optimizations (power management, hardware decoding). However, native controls vary wildly across browsers, offer no customization, and don't support advanced features like ABR quality selection, chapter markers, or mini-player. A fully custom player (custom controls over a controlslist-hidden video element) provides complete UX control but requires reimplementing accessibility, platform behaviors (iOS fullscreen, Android PiP), and handling the complexity of MSE/EME APIs. The chosen approach uses a custom player for the UI layer while leveraging native capabilities (MSE for buffering, EME for DRM, native fullscreen API) underneath.

HLS vs DASH protocol choice affects codec support, DRM support, and implementation complexity. HLS is natively supported on Safari (no library needed) and has broad CDN support, but requires hls.js for other browsers and has historically been limited to H.264/AAC (though CMAF with HEVC/AV1 support is emerging). DASH is more flexible with codec support and has better adaptive streaming specifications, but requires a JavaScript library on all browsers and has less mature tooling. Many production systems support both, using HLS for Safari and DASH for other browsers, at the cost of encoding and serving both formats.

The buffer size tradeoff balances rebuffering prevention against memory usage and seek latency. Large buffers (60+ seconds) virtually eliminate rebuffering but consume significant memory (150MB+ for high-quality video), waste bandwidth if the user stops watching, and make quality switches slower (the buffer must drain before higher-quality segments appear). Small buffers (5-10 seconds) use less memory and enable faster quality adaptation but risk rebuffering on variable networks. The adaptive approach — starting with a 15-second buffer and expanding or contracting based on network stability — adds implementation complexity but optimizes for both scenarios.`,
  },
  {
    id: "sd-ecommerce",
    title: "E-Commerce Frontend",
    difficulty: "Senior",
    category: "System Design",
    tags: [
      "product-listing",
      "cart",
      "checkout",
      "search",
      "filters",
      "performance",
      "seo",
    ],
    requirements: `The e-commerce frontend must support the complete shopping experience: product discovery (search, browse categories, filters, sorting), product detail viewing (images, descriptions, variants, reviews, related products), cart management (add/remove items, quantity adjustment, saved items), and checkout flow (shipping, payment, order confirmation). The system must handle a catalog of 1 million+ products with performant search and filtering.

Product listing pages must support faceted filtering (price range, brand, color, size, rating, availability) with real-time count updates as filters are applied. Sorting options include relevance, price (low/high), popularity, rating, and newest. The listing must use either infinite scroll or paginated navigation (configurable per user preference). Each product card shows image, title, price (with sale price and discount percentage), rating stars, "Add to Cart" button, and wishlist toggle.

The cart must persist across sessions (logged-in users: server-side, anonymous users: localStorage). Cart operations must be instant (optimistic UI) with background server sync. The checkout must support multiple payment methods (credit card via Stripe Elements, PayPal, Apple Pay, Google Pay), address autocomplete, real-time shipping cost calculation, coupon/promo code application, and order summary with itemized totals. The entire experience must be SEO-optimized with server-side rendering for product pages and proper structured data (JSON-LD) for search engine indexing.`,

    constraints: `Product listing page must achieve a Largest Contentful Paint (LCP) under 2.5 seconds and First Input Delay (FID) under 100ms (Core Web Vitals targets). Product images must use modern formats (WebP/AVIF) with proper srcset for responsive sizing. The initial HTML payload must contain above-the-fold content via SSR for SEO and perceived performance.

The search system must return results within 200ms of user input (with debouncing). Filter changes must update the product list within 500ms. The cart must support up to 100 items. The checkout payment form must be PCI-DSS compliant — credit card data never touches the application server (handled via Stripe Elements iframe or equivalent tokenization).

The JavaScript bundle for the product listing page must not exceed 200KB gzipped. Critical CSS must be inlined for above-the-fold rendering. The system must support 10,000 concurrent users during peak events (flash sales, holiday traffic). Product URLs must be human-readable and SEO-friendly (/products/blue-running-shoes-nike-air-max rather than /products/12345).`,

    highLevelArchitecture: `The architecture uses Next.js (or a similar SSR framework) for server-side rendering, static generation, and API routes. The application is structured into four main flows: Discovery (search and browse), Product Detail, Cart, and Checkout. Each flow has its own page-level code splitting, data fetching strategy, and caching behavior.

The data architecture uses a BFF (Backend for Frontend) pattern. The Next.js API routes serve as the BFF, aggregating data from multiple backend services (Product Service, Search Service, Cart Service, Checkout Service, User Service) into frontend-optimized payloads. This eliminates over-fetching and under-fetching while hiding backend complexity from the client. The BFF caches product catalog data aggressively (Redis with 5-minute TTL for product listings, 1-hour TTL for product details) while keeping cart and checkout data real-time.

Client-side state management separates server state (product data, search results) from client state (cart contents, UI preferences). Server state uses React Query with SSR hydration — data fetched during SSR is dehydrated into the HTML and rehydrated on the client, avoiding redundant client-side fetches. Client state uses Zustand with localStorage persistence for the cart (anonymous users) and server sync for authenticated users. The search and filter state is encoded in URL parameters for shareability, SEO, and browser history support.`,

    componentArchitecture: `The page components follow the Next.js app router pattern. ProductListingPage (SSR + client hydration) contains BreadcrumbNav, SearchBar, FilterSidebar (collapsible on mobile), ActiveFilters (chip display), SortSelector, ProductGrid, and PaginationControls. ProductDetailPage (SSR with ISR) contains ImageGallery, ProductInfo (title, price, variant selector), AddToCartSection, ProductTabs (description, specs, reviews), and RelatedProducts. CartPage (client-only) contains CartItemList, CartSummary, PromoCodeInput, and CheckoutButton. CheckoutPage (client-only) contains CheckoutStepper, ShippingForm, PaymentForm, OrderSummary, and PlaceOrderButton.

ProductGrid renders ProductCard components in a responsive CSS Grid layout (2 columns mobile, 3 tablet, 4 desktop). Each ProductCard is a standalone component with ProductImage (lazy-loaded with blur placeholder), ProductInfo (title, price, rating), QuickAddButton, and WishlistToggle. ProductCard is memoized by product ID and price (which may change during sales).

The FilterSidebar contains FilterGroup components for each facet (PriceRangeFilter with dual-handle slider, CheckboxFilterGroup for brands/colors/sizes, RatingFilter with star selectors). Each filter component manages its local draft state (user interacts) and commits to the URL parameters on apply, triggering a new product fetch. The ImageGallery on the product detail page uses a Swiper-based carousel with zoom capability, thumbnail navigation, and pinch-to-zoom on mobile.`,

    stateManagement: `Product data uses React Query with server-side dehydration. The query key for product listings is ["products", { category, filters, sort, page }], ensuring automatic cache invalidation when any parameter changes. Product detail pages use ["product", productId] with a longer stale time (5 minutes vs 30 seconds for listings). Search results use ["search", { query, filters }] with a short stale time (10 seconds) for relevance freshness.

Cart state uses Zustand with a persistence middleware that syncs to localStorage for anonymous users and to the server for authenticated users. The cart store shape is: { items: CartItem[], addItem(product, quantity, variant), removeItem(itemId), updateQuantity(itemId, quantity), applyCoupon(code), clearCart(), syncStatus: "synced" | "syncing" | "error" }. Each CartItem contains: { id, productId, title, image, price, salePrice, quantity, variant: { size, color }, maxQuantity }. Optimistic updates modify the local cart immediately, with a debounced sync to the server (500ms) that reconciles any conflicts (e.g., product went out of stock between the local add and the server sync).

Filter and sort state lives entirely in URL search parameters, managed by the useSearchParams hook. This approach makes every filter combination bookmarkable and shareable, supports browser back/forward for filter changes, and enables SSR to render the correct filtered results on direct URL access. A custom useFilters hook abstracts URL parameter parsing, serialization, and navigation, providing a clean API to FilterSidebar and ActiveFilters components.`,

    apiDesign: `The BFF API consolidates backend microservices. GET /api/products?category={cat}&brand={brands}&price_min={min}&price_max={max}&color={colors}&size={sizes}&rating={min_rating}&sort={sort}&page={page}&limit={limit} returns { products: ProductSummary[], facets: Record<string, Array<{ value: string, count: number }>>, pagination: { page, limit, total, totalPages }, meta: { appliedFilters, sort } }. The facets object contains updated count values for each filter option given the current selection, enabling real-time count display.

GET /api/products/{slug} returns the full product detail with { product: ProductDetail, relatedProducts: ProductSummary[], breadcrumbs: BreadcrumbItem[] }. ProductDetail includes images (multiple sizes per image for srcset), variants (size/color matrix with availability), pricing (regular, sale, percentage off), reviews summary (average rating, count, distribution), and structured data (JSON-LD for search engines).

Cart API: GET /api/cart returns the current cart. POST /api/cart/items { productId, variantId, quantity } adds an item. PATCH /api/cart/items/{itemId} { quantity } updates quantity. DELETE /api/cart/items/{itemId} removes an item. POST /api/cart/coupon { code } applies a coupon. Checkout API: POST /api/checkout/shipping-rates { address, items } returns available shipping options. POST /api/checkout/create-payment-intent { items, shipping, coupon } creates a Stripe PaymentIntent. POST /api/checkout/complete { paymentIntentId, shippingAddress, billingAddress } finalizes the order.`,

    caching: `Caching is the most critical aspect of e-commerce performance. The CDN (CloudFront, Fastly) caches SSR-rendered product listing pages with a 60-second TTL, using Surrogate-Key headers for targeted cache purging when product data changes (price update, stock change). Product detail pages use ISR (Incremental Static Regeneration) with a 5-minute revalidation period — pages are pre-rendered at build time for popular products and generated on-demand for long-tail products.

Product images use aggressive CDN caching (Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable) with content-addressed URLs. The image transformation service (Cloudinary, imgix) generates responsive sizes on first request and caches them at the edge. The srcset approach ensures each device downloads only the appropriate image size, reducing bandwidth by 40-60% compared to serving a single large image.

Client-side caching via React Query provides stale-while-revalidate semantics. When the user navigates from a product list to a product detail and back, the product list renders from cache immediately while revalidating in the background. The cart uses optimistic local state with server reconciliation, ensuring the cart badge count updates instantly after "Add to Cart" clicks. Search suggestions are cached in memory with a 30-second TTL for prefix-based lookups, reducing API calls during rapid typing. Browser back/forward navigation uses bfcache-friendly patterns (no unload event listeners, no Cache-Control: no-store on HTML) for instant back navigation.`,

    performance: `Core Web Vitals optimization drives every architectural decision. LCP is optimized by inlining critical CSS (above-the-fold styles extracted at build time), preloading the hero product image via Link: rel=preload, and server-side rendering the initial product grid. CLS is minimized by reserving space for images (aspect-ratio CSS property), ads, and dynamically loaded content. FID is optimized by minimizing main-thread work — heavy operations (filter count calculation, search indexing) run in Web Workers.

Product listing performance uses progressive rendering. The server renders the first 12 products with SSR, and the client hydrates and fetches additional products. Images use loading="lazy" with a priority exception for above-the-fold images (loading="eager" and fetchpriority="high"). Product cards use content-visibility: auto for off-screen rendering optimization. The filter sidebar on desktop uses a virtualized checkbox list when brands exceed 50 entries.

Checkout performance is critical for conversion. The payment form (Stripe Elements) is pre-initialized when the user enters the checkout flow, not when they reach the payment step. Address autocomplete uses debounced API calls with local caching of recent addresses. The "Place Order" button provides immediate tactile feedback (loading state) while the payment processing happens asynchronously. The order confirmation page is pre-rendered with the order data passed via server-side redirect, avoiding a client-side fetch on the confirmation page.`,

    security: `PCI-DSS compliance is non-negotiable for handling payment data. Credit card input uses Stripe Elements (or equivalent), which renders in an iframe from Stripe's domain. The application never receives raw card numbers — only a tokenized PaymentMethod ID. The checkout page uses Content-Security-Policy headers that restrict iframes to Stripe's domain and block inline scripts.

Authentication uses HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite=Lax cookies for session management. SameSite=Lax (not Strict) is needed for OAuth redirect flows and payment provider callbacks. CSRF protection uses the synchronizer token pattern — a CSRF token is embedded in the SSR HTML and included in all mutating API requests. Cart manipulation and checkout endpoints enforce CSRF validation.

Product data integrity prevents price manipulation. The server validates prices at checkout against the current product catalog — the cart's item prices are informational for display but authoritative pricing comes from the server at order creation. Coupon validation is server-side with rate limiting (5 coupon attempts per minute per user) to prevent brute-force code guessing. User reviews are moderated and sanitized against XSS before rendering. The search input sanitizes against NoSQL injection and script injection patterns. Rate limiting on the search API (100 requests per minute per IP) prevents scraping and abuse.`,

    accessibility: `Product listing pages follow the WAI-ARIA grid pattern for product cards, with each card as a focusable article element containing semantically structured content. The filter sidebar uses standard form elements (checkboxes, radio buttons, range inputs) with proper label associations and fieldset/legend grouping. Filter application announcements use aria-live="polite" to communicate results: "Showing 24 of 150 products matching your filters."

Product images use descriptive alt text generated from product attributes: "Nike Air Max 90 in Blue, side view" rather than generic "Product image." The image gallery supports keyboard navigation (arrow keys to cycle images, Enter to zoom). The variant selector (size, color) uses role="radiogroup" with visual and textual indicators for availability: "Size 10 — Out of stock" via aria-disabled and aria-label.

The checkout flow is structured with a step indicator using aria-current="step" and descriptive labels. Form inputs use autocomplete attributes for browser autofill (autocomplete="shipping street-address", "cc-number"). Validation errors are announced via aria-describedby linking to inline error messages. The payment form accessibility is handled by Stripe Elements, which implements ARIA patterns within its iframe. The order confirmation page includes an aria-live="assertive" announcement: "Order placed successfully! Order number #12345." Price display includes aria-label with full context: "Sale price $79.99, was $99.99, you save 20 percent."`,

    errorHandling: `Product listing errors use a graceful degradation strategy. If the product API fails, the page shows cached results (stale-while-revalidate) with a "Showing cached results" banner, or falls back to a category-level cache. If no cache is available, a friendly error page shows popular categories and a search bar: "We're having trouble loading products — try searching or browse popular categories."

Cart synchronization errors are handled without interrupting the shopping experience. If the server cart sync fails, the local cart state is preserved and sync retries with exponential backoff. Conflict resolution handles common scenarios: item went out of stock (show notification, move to "saved for later"), price changed (show notification with new price, let user confirm), quantity exceeds available stock (adjust quantity to maximum, notify user). These conflicts are resolved during the checkout API call, and any issues are displayed on the cart page before the user proceeds to payment.

Checkout and payment errors are critical for revenue. Payment failures (declined card, insufficient funds, fraud detection) show specific, actionable messages: "Your card was declined — please try a different payment method" rather than generic "Payment failed." Network errors during payment are handled carefully — the client uses Stripe's payment intent status to determine if the payment succeeded (avoiding double charges). The order confirmation page handles the edge case where the order was created but the confirmation redirect failed — it checks for pending orders and displays the most recent one.`,

    offlineStrategy: `The e-commerce frontend implements a service-worker-based offline strategy optimized for browsing continuity. Product listing pages and product detail pages that the user has visited are cached using a stale-while-revalidate strategy, enabling offline browsing of previously viewed products. The service worker caches the application shell (HTML, CSS, JS, fonts) with a cache-first strategy for instant loading.

The cart is fully functional offline via localStorage persistence. Users can add items to the cart, adjust quantities, and apply saved coupons while offline. Cart modifications are queued and synced when connectivity resumes. The checkout flow, however, requires connectivity and shows a clear message: "Checkout requires an internet connection — your cart is saved and will be available when you're back online."

For browsing, the offline experience includes a search index of recently viewed products (stored in IndexedDB), enabling offline search within the cached catalog. Product images for viewed products are cached by the service worker. When the user attempts to access a non-cached product, the offline page shows: "This product isn't available offline" with a button to save the URL for later. A "Save for offline" feature on product pages explicitly caches the product data and images for offline access, useful for users who know they'll be offline (e.g., shopping on an airplane).`,

    scalability: `At 10x traffic (flash sales, holiday events), the frontend architecture must handle CDN cache thundering herd and dynamic content scaling. Product listing pages should be served from CDN cache with stale-while-revalidate behavior — during cache refresh, stale content is served to prevent thundering herd on the origin. The SSR infrastructure must auto-scale horizontally, with pre-warming for anticipated traffic spikes. Client-side, the search debounce timer should dynamically increase during high traffic (detected via slow API responses) to reduce backend load.

At 100x catalog (10 million products), search and filter performance becomes critical. The faceted filter system must use pre-computed aggregations rather than real-time counting — updating aggregation tables when products change rather than calculating counts per request. The client-side filter UI should request aggregations lazily (only for expanded filter groups) rather than all facets upfront. Product listing pages beyond the first 5 pages should be noindex to prevent search engine crawl budget waste.

At global scale, the frontend must support multi-region deployment with locale-specific content. This includes multi-currency pricing (calculated server-side, cached per locale), translated product descriptions, locale-specific availability, and regional CDN serving. The SSR rendering pipeline should support edge-side rendering (Cloudflare Workers, Vercel Edge) for globally low TTFB. The checkout must handle country-specific payment methods (iDEAL for Netherlands, Klarna for Sweden, UPI for India) with dynamic payment method loading based on the user's billing country.`,

    tradeoffs: `SSR vs SSG vs CSR for product pages involves significant tradeoffs. SSR provides fresh content on every request and full SEO support but adds server load and TTFB latency. SSG provides the fastest TTFB but can't handle frequently changing data (prices, stock) without revalidation. CSR loads fastest for repeat visits (cached JS) but has poor SEO and empty initial HTML. The hybrid approach (ISR with SSR fallback) provides the best balance: popular products are pre-rendered and cached, with periodic revalidation for freshness; long-tail products use SSR on first access and then cache for subsequent visitors.

The BFF pattern vs direct API access tradeoff affects development velocity and performance. The BFF adds a layer between the frontend and backend microservices, requiring maintenance and deployment. However, it eliminates waterfall API calls (one BFF request replaces 3-4 microservice calls), enables frontend-optimized data shapes, and provides a single point for caching and error handling. Without BFF, the frontend either makes multiple parallel requests (slower, more error-prone) or the backend provides frontend-specific endpoints (coupling backend to frontend needs).

Optimistic cart updates vs confirmed cart updates involve UX and data consistency tradeoffs. Optimistic updates (showing the item in the cart immediately) provide the best UX but risk showing incorrect state (item out of stock, price changed). Confirmed updates (spinner until server responds) are always consistent but feel slow (200-500ms delay per action). The hybrid approach (optimistic for quantity changes and non-critical updates, confirmed for add-to-cart with real-time stock check) balances speed and accuracy but adds conditional logic in the cart state management.`,
  },
  {
    id: "sd-collaborative-editor",
    title: "Collaborative Text Editor",
    difficulty: "Senior",
    category: "System Design",
    tags: [
      "real-time-collaboration",
      "crdt",
      "operational-transform",
      "conflict-resolution",
      "cursor-sync",
      "rich-text",
    ],
    requirements: `The collaborative text editor must support simultaneous real-time editing by multiple users (up to 50 concurrent collaborators per document) with sub-second synchronization of changes. The editor must handle rich text formatting (bold, italic, underline, strikethrough, headings, lists, blockquotes, code blocks, links, images, tables), with formatting operations that are also collaboratively synchronized.

Each collaborator's cursor and selection must be visible to all other collaborators in real-time, rendered with a unique color and a name label. The system must handle conflict resolution seamlessly — when two users type at the same position simultaneously, both edits must be preserved in a deterministic order without data loss. The editor must maintain a complete version history, allowing users to view the document at any point in time, compare versions, and restore previous versions.

Presence features include: a list of active collaborators (with their online status and current cursor position), @mention functionality (type @ to see a list of collaborators and notify them), and collaborative comments anchored to text ranges (with threads and resolution). The editor must support both online collaborative editing and offline editing with automatic merge when connectivity resumes.`,

    constraints: `Edit operations must propagate to all connected clients within 500ms under normal conditions. The editor must handle up to 50 concurrent users editing the same paragraph without visible lag or cursor jumping. The conflict resolution algorithm must guarantee eventual consistency — all clients converge to the same document state regardless of event ordering.

The document model must support documents up to 500,000 characters (approximately 100 pages) without performance degradation. Undo/redo must work correctly in a collaborative context — each user has their own undo stack that reverses only their operations without affecting other users' edits. The editor's JavaScript bundle should not exceed 250KB gzipped (including the CRDT/OT library).

The version history must store at least 1000 versions per document, with efficient diff computation between any two versions. The real-time collaboration connection must recover from network interruptions within 5 seconds, replaying any missed operations without user intervention. Memory usage should stay below 200MB even for large documents with extensive editing history.`,

    highLevelArchitecture: `The architecture is built around a CRDT (Conflict-free Replicated Data Type) engine that manages the document model and ensures eventual consistency. The choice of CRDT over Operational Transform (OT) is driven by the requirement for offline editing support — CRDTs can merge changes from disconnected clients without a central server to transform operations, while OT requires a centralized transform server.

The system consists of four layers: the Editor UI (ProseMirror or Slate-based rich text editor), the CRDT Engine (Yjs or Automerge), the Sync Layer (WebSocket + awareness protocol for real-time sync), and the Persistence Layer (IndexedDB for local state, server-side document storage). The Editor UI captures user input as editor transactions, which are translated into CRDT operations by a binding layer. The CRDT Engine applies operations to its internal document model (a tree CRDT for rich text, tracking character positions with unique IDs rather than numerical indices) and generates update messages.

The Sync Layer uses a WebSocket connection to broadcast CRDT updates to all connected clients via a sync server. The sync server acts as a relay and persistence point — it doesn't transform operations (the CRDT handles conflict resolution) but stores the document state and relays updates between clients. When a new client connects, it receives the current document state (either as a full CRDT state snapshot or as a sequence of updates since its last known state). The awareness protocol, running alongside the CRDT sync, handles ephemeral collaborative data: cursor positions, selections, and user presence.`,

    componentArchitecture: `The root component is CollaborativeEditorPage, containing DocumentHeader (title, collaborator avatars, share button, version history button), EditorContainer (the main editing area), CommentsSidebar (anchored comments panel), and PresenceBar (list of active collaborators with cursor positions).

EditorContainer wraps the core editor component (ProseMirror EditorView or Slate Editable) and adds collaborative overlays: RemoteCursors (SVG or absolutely-positioned elements showing other users' cursors with colored labels), RemoteSelections (highlighted text ranges for other users' selections), and CommentMarkers (highlighted text ranges anchored to comments). The editor itself renders the document content using a schema that defines node types (paragraph, heading, list, code block, table) and mark types (bold, italic, link).

The FormatToolbar is a floating or fixed toolbar containing formatting buttons organized by group: text style (bold, italic, underline), paragraph style (heading level, list type, blockquote), insert (link, image, table, code block), and collaboration (comment, @mention). Each button's active state reflects the current selection's formatting. The MentionPopover appears when the user types "@", showing a filtered list of collaborators. The VersionHistoryPanel (slide-out panel) shows a timeline of document versions with author attribution, diff view, and restore button.`,

    stateManagement: `The document state is managed entirely by the CRDT engine (Yjs), not by React state. This is a critical architectural decision — the CRDT maintains the source of truth as a specialized data structure (Y.Doc) that supports concurrent modifications and automatic conflict resolution. The React editor component observes CRDT changes and re-renders accordingly, but React state (useState, Redux, etc.) is not used for document content.

The Yjs document model represents rich text as a Y.XmlFragment containing Y.XmlElement nodes (paragraphs, headings, etc.) with Y.XmlText leaf nodes. Each character in the text has a unique identifier (a Lamport timestamp + client ID pair) that provides a total ordering even across disconnected clients. When two users type at the same position, the character IDs determine the final order deterministically.

Non-document state is split between collaboration state and UI state. Collaboration state (presence, cursors, selections) uses the Yjs awareness protocol — each client broadcasts its awareness state (cursor position, user info, selection range) to all other clients via the WebSocket. This state is ephemeral and not persisted. UI state (toolbar state, sidebar visibility, active tab) uses local React state. The undo/redo system uses Yjs's UndoManager, which tracks operations per client — pressing Ctrl+Z reverses only the current user's operations, correctly handling the case where other users' operations are interleaved in the document timeline.`,

    apiDesign: `The sync protocol uses a WebSocket connection to a collaboration server. The initial handshake sends: { type: "join", docId: string, userId: string, clientId: number }. The server responds with: { type: "sync", state: Uint8Array (encoded Y.Doc state vector) } or { type: "sync-step-1", stateVector: Uint8Array } initiating a two-step sync protocol where the client sends its state vector, the server responds with missing updates, and vice versa.

Ongoing synchronization uses binary-encoded CRDT update messages: { type: "update", update: Uint8Array }. These are compact binary representations of the changes (a single character insertion is typically 10-20 bytes). The awareness protocol sends: { type: "awareness", states: Map<clientId, { cursor: { anchor: RelativePosition, head: RelativePosition }, user: { name: string, color: string }, selection: { anchor: RelativePosition, head: RelativePosition } }> }. Relative positions (a Yjs concept) reference content rather than indices, ensuring cursor positions remain valid even as the document is modified.

REST endpoints handle non-real-time operations. GET /api/documents/{id} returns document metadata and initial state. GET /api/documents/{id}/versions returns version history. POST /api/documents/{id}/versions/{versionId}/restore restores a version. POST /api/documents/{id}/comments creates a comment anchored to a text range (using relative positions). GET /api/documents/{id}/comments returns all comments with their thread replies. The document is periodically snapshot to the server (every 30 seconds or on significant changes) via a REST endpoint, providing backup persistence independent of the WebSocket connection.`,

    caching: `The CRDT document state is persisted to IndexedDB after every update using a Yjs IndexedDB persistence provider. This means the full document state survives browser restarts without server round-trips. When the user opens a document, the local IndexedDB state loads instantly, rendering the document. The WebSocket connection then syncs any changes that occurred while the user was away, merging them into the local state via the CRDT's automatic conflict resolution.

The version history uses a compact storage format. Rather than storing full document snapshots for each version, the server stores the CRDT update log — a sequence of binary-encoded updates. Any historical version can be reconstructed by replaying updates up to that point. For efficiency, the server periodically compacts the update log into a full snapshot + subsequent updates (similar to database WAL checkpointing), keeping the snapshot count manageable (one per 100 updates).

Awareness state (cursors, selections, presence) is not cached — it's ephemeral and regenerated on each connection. Document metadata (title, collaborators, permissions) is cached in React Query with a 30-second stale time. Comment data is cached with a 1-minute stale time and updated via WebSocket events when new comments are added. The offline cache in IndexedDB includes the full document state, pending local updates that haven't been synced to the server, and comment anchors (so comments display correctly even offline).`,

    performance: `Editor rendering performance is critical because every keystroke must be responsive. The ProseMirror/Slate rendering engine uses a virtual DOM diffing approach optimized for text editing — only the affected paragraph is re-rendered when text changes, not the entire document. For large documents, a viewport-based rendering optimization is applied — paragraphs far from the viewport are rendered as lightweight placeholder elements with correct heights, replacing with full content as the user scrolls.

CRDT operation performance for Yjs is O(log n) for insertions and deletions (using a skip-list-based internal structure), where n is the document length. For a 500,000-character document, each operation takes < 1ms. The binary encoding of updates is compact — a typical keystroke generates a 15-20 byte update message, and WebSocket overhead is minimal. Cursor position broadcasts are throttled to 50ms intervals to prevent network flooding during rapid cursor movement.

Remote cursor and selection rendering uses CSS transforms for positioning (GPU-composited, no layout thrashing) and transitions for smooth movement animation. When 50 users are connected, cursor updates arrive at high frequency — the rendering batches cursor position updates using requestAnimationFrame, applying at most one position update per cursor per frame. The awareness state uses a Map data structure for O(1) cursor lookups. Large paste operations (e.g., pasting 10,000 characters) are handled by Yjs as a single transaction, generating one update message rather than 10,000 individual character insertions.`,

    security: `Document access control is enforced at the WebSocket connection level. The collaboration server validates the user's authentication token and document permissions before accepting the connection. Permission levels include: viewer (can read, can't edit), commenter (can read and comment, can't edit), editor (full edit access), and owner (edit + manage permissions + delete). The server rejects update messages from clients without editor permission.

Content sanitization is critical because the editor supports rich text including HTML-like structures. The document schema (ProseMirror schema) defines a strict whitelist of allowed node types and attributes. Any content that doesn't conform to the schema is rejected at the editor level, preventing XSS through crafted document content. The server also validates updates against the schema before persisting them.

Collaboration data is transmitted over WSS (secure WebSocket). For sensitive documents, end-to-end encryption can be layered on top of the CRDT sync protocol — the CRDT update bytes are encrypted with a shared document key before transmission, and the server only sees encrypted blobs. However, this prevents server-side features like search and version preview (since the server can't read the content). The version history and document storage use server-side encryption at rest. Comment content is sanitized using the same schema rules as the main editor content. Share links use capability-based URLs with revocable tokens rather than user-based permission checks.`,

    accessibility: `The rich text editor provides comprehensive keyboard support for all editing operations. Formatting shortcuts follow standard conventions: Ctrl+B for bold, Ctrl+I for italic, Ctrl+K for link insertion. Block-level formatting uses Markdown shortcuts: typing "## " at the start of a line converts to a heading, "- " creates a list item, "> " creates a blockquote, and "\\\`\\\`\\\`" creates a code block. These shortcuts are documented in an accessible keyboard shortcut help dialog (opened via Ctrl+/).

Collaborative cursor labels use aria-hidden="true" (they're decorative for sighted users) while the collaborator list in the PresenceBar provides the authoritative collaborator information for screen readers. Document structure is conveyed through proper heading hierarchy, list semantics, and blockquote roles. The editor uses contenteditable, which provides built-in screen reader support for text navigation, selection, and editing.

The @mention popover implements the combobox pattern (role="combobox" on the trigger, role="listbox" on the dropdown) with proper aria-activedescendant management. Comments are accessible via a dedicated sidebar that can be navigated with Tab, with each comment thread containing proper heading structure and ARIA roles. The version history panel uses a timeline metaphor with each version as a focusable item, and the diff view uses accessible color coding (additions and deletions are communicated through prefixed symbols, not just color, following diff conventions).`,

    errorHandling: `CRDT synchronization errors are categorized by severity. Connection loss is the most common — the system shows a subtle "Connecting..." indicator and buffers local edits. On reconnection, the CRDT sync protocol replays missed updates, and the local buffer is transmitted. No user intervention is required, and no edits are lost. If reconnection fails after 30 seconds, the indicator changes to "Working offline — changes will sync when connected" with a manual retry button.

Document corruption (invalid CRDT state) is rare but handled defensively. The CRDT library includes internal consistency checks. If an inconsistency is detected, the system attempts recovery by re-syncing the full document state from the server. If recovery fails, the system saves the local state to a recovery file and prompts the user to reload the page. The server maintains periodic snapshots, enabling point-in-time recovery for the worst-case scenario.

Permission errors (e.g., the user's edit access is revoked while editing) are handled by the server rejecting update messages. The client receives a permission-denied message and transitions the editor to read-only mode with a notification: "Your editing access has been revoked. You can still view the document." Any unsaved local changes are preserved in a conflict copy that the user can manually merge if access is restored. Comment creation/edit failures use optimistic rollback with inline retry buttons, and the comment's local state shows a "not synced" indicator until server confirmation.`,

    offlineStrategy: `Offline editing is a first-class feature enabled by the CRDT architecture. When the user goes offline, the editor continues to function fully — typing, formatting, adding comments, and all editing operations work locally. The CRDT engine applies operations to the local Y.Doc, and the IndexedDB persistence provider saves the state after each change. The CRDT's conflict-free properties guarantee that offline edits can be merged with concurrent online edits from other users without conflicts.

When connectivity resumes, the Yjs sync protocol handles the merge automatically. The client's local state vector (a compact representation of all operations the client has seen) is compared with the server's state vector. Missing updates are exchanged bidirectionally — the server sends operations the client missed, and the client sends operations the server hasn't seen. The CRDT's mathematical properties ensure that the merge result is identical regardless of the order in which updates are applied, achieving strong eventual consistency.

The offline experience includes visual indicators: an "Offline" badge in the header, disabled collaboration features (presence list, remote cursors are hidden since they're stale), and a "Pending sync" indicator showing the number of local changes waiting to sync. The @mention feature works offline using a cached collaborator list. Comments created offline are stored locally and synced on reconnection. The version history is limited to locally cached versions while offline. A conflict resolution UI is not needed because the CRDT resolves all conflicts automatically — but a "Recent changes" panel highlights edits that were merged from other users during the offline period, so the user can review what changed.`,

    scalability: `At 10x document size (5 million characters), the CRDT's internal data structure must handle large state efficiently. Yjs's skip-list structure provides O(log n) operations even at this scale, but the initial sync (loading the full document state) may take 1-2 seconds for very large documents. The solution is to implement lazy loading: the document is split into sections (pages or chapters), and only the visible section's CRDT state is fully loaded. Adjacent sections are pre-loaded in the background. The full document state is assembled lazily for operations like search-and-replace that span sections.

At 50 concurrent editors (the stated maximum), the WebSocket message volume scales quadratically for awareness updates (each client must receive updates from all others). The optimization is to throttle awareness updates based on distance — cursor movements far from the viewport are sent at lower frequency (500ms vs 50ms for nearby cursors). The server can also implement awareness batching, collecting all cursor updates over a 100ms window and broadcasting them as a single message rather than individually.

At 100x document count (thousands of collaborative documents in an organization), the server architecture must handle many concurrent rooms. Each document's collaboration is a separate room on the sync server, with independent CRDT state and WebSocket connections. The server uses a room-based memory management strategy — inactive rooms (no connected clients) are evicted from memory after 5 minutes, with their state persisted to disk. Active rooms are sharded across multiple server instances using consistent hashing on the document ID, with a Redis-based room registry for routing clients to the correct server instance.`,

    tradeoffs: `CRDT vs OT is the most fundamental architectural decision. CRDTs provide mathematical guarantees of conflict-free merging without a central server, enabling offline editing and peer-to-peer sync. However, CRDTs have higher memory overhead (each character carries a unique ID — Yjs uses ~2x the raw text size), more complex tombstone management (deleted characters must be retained as tombstones for consistency, requiring periodic garbage collection), and the algorithms are harder to understand and debug. OT has lower memory overhead and more mature tooling (Google Docs uses OT) but requires a centralized transform server, making offline editing significantly harder and adding single-point-of-failure risk.

ProseMirror vs Slate vs custom editor engine is a rich text framework tradeoff. ProseMirror has a robust, well-tested document model with a mature ecosystem and good CRDT bindings (yjs-prosemirror). Its functional, immutable design makes it predictable but has a steeper learning curve. Slate has a more React-native API and is easier to customize, but its document model has historically had instability issues. A custom engine provides full control but requires enormous development effort for features like text shaping, IME handling, and cross-browser compatibility. ProseMirror is chosen for its reliability and CRDT compatibility.

Real-time cursor broadcasting vs on-demand cursor fetching involves bandwidth and UX tradeoffs. Broadcasting cursors in real-time (every 50ms during movement) provides a lively collaborative feeling but generates significant network traffic with many users (50 users × 20 cursor updates/second × 100 bytes = 100KB/s of cursor data alone). On-demand fetching (fetching cursor positions only when the user looks at a specific document section) saves bandwidth but makes the collaboration feel less dynamic. The throttled broadcasting approach (50ms for nearby cursors, 500ms for distant cursors, no updates for offscreen cursors) balances both concerns.`,
  },
];
